          {
            "raw_output": "/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/repro_scratch.py:12:    torch.set_float32_matmul_precision('medium')\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/repro_scratch.py:32:        max_number_of_samples=6,\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/CLAUDE.md:41:# Quick sanity run: cap the dataset with max_samples in the YAML, then\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/CLAUDE.md:55:Run the test suite with `pytest tests/ -v` (currently `tests/test_lora_setup.py`, which is CPU-only and fast). For changes not covered by tests, verify by running the training script with `max_samples` capped (see above).\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/CLAUDE.md:105:`HelioSpectformer1D` derives the backbone's `nglo` argument from `pooling` internally (`1` for `class_token`, `0` otherwise) \u2014 it is not a config field. `ModelConfig`/`TrainingConfig` also validate several other cross-field invariants at config-load time (`img_size` vs `patch_size`, `spectral_blocks`/`checkpoint_layers` vs `depth`, `time_embedding.time_dim` vs `data.time_delta_input_minutes`, `training.deterministic` vs `model.learned_flow`, and `model.learned_flow` vs `time_embedding.type`) \u2014 see `ModelConfig.__post_init__` and `TrainingConfig.__post_init__` in `workshop_infrastructure/configs.py` for the current list, and add new ones there rather than leaving them as documentation-only footguns.\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/CLAUDE.md:111:  \u2193 CSV index (path, timestamp, label)  \u2190  data/indices/\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/CLAUDE.md:135:**Reproducibility.** `training.seed` and `training.deterministic` (`false` | `warn` | `true`, **default `false`** for throughput \u2014 determinism costs ~20% wall time) control it. Results are therefore NOT reproducible out of the box; `warn` is the setting to use when comparing runs. `3_finetune_template_1D.py` sets `CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG=:4096:8` **before importing torch** \u2014 this is required for deterministic cuBLAS and is inert if moved after the import, so do not \"tidy\" it into the other imports. The notebooks' first cell does the same. `build_helio_dataloaders()` passes an explicit `generator` and `worker_init_fn`; without them the shuffle order depends on ambient global RNG state. `deterministic: true` is incompatible with `model.learned_flow: true` (`F.grid_sample` has no deterministic CUDA backward); `TrainingConfig.__post_init__` rejects that combination at config-load time.\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/README.md:154:| `0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb` | How SDO data is indexed, loaded, and normalized; what a sample dict looks like |\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/README.md:182:Set `max_samples: 10` in the YAML during development to cap the dataset size for fast iteration.\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/README.md:240:**Reproducibility is one config key away.** `training.deterministic` defaults to `false` for throughput \u2014 determinism costs about 20% of wall time \u2014 so two identical runs may disagree. Set it to `warn` whenever you need to attribute a change in results to your edit rather than to drift; that is the setting to use for any ablation or before/after comparison. The machinery behind it pins three things that are easy to leave loose: `torch.use_deterministic_algorithms` via Lightning's `deterministic` flag, `cudnn.benchmark` (autotuning picks algorithms by timing, which drifts), and the train DataLoader's shuffle generator and worker seeds \u2014 a bare `shuffle=True` seeds itself from ambient global RNG state, so unrelated code that draws a random number silently reshuffles your epochs. The seed and the data order stay pinned either way. Note that reproducibility is not significance: with `max_samples: 10` the result is still noise, just the same noise each time.\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py:39:    (``random.sample`` / ``random.random``), and downstream datasets commonly use NumPy.\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py:58:        # Temporal sampling\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py:61:        n_input_timestamps=cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim,\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py:128:            a bare ``shuffle=True`` seeds its sampler from whatever the global torch RNG\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py:146:        pin_memory=True,\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py:153:        loader_kwargs[\"persistent_workers\"] = True\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/models/transformer_ls.py:141:            )  # Changed when experimenting with mixed precision (Johannes S.)\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/models/transformer_ls.py:198:        )  # Changed when experimenting with mixed precision (Johannes S.)\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/models/transformer_ls.py:216:                )  # Changed when experimenting with mixed precision (Johannes S.)\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/models/transformer_ls.py:247:            )  # Changed when experimenting with mixed precision (Johannes S.)\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/models/transformer_ls.py:265:        that corresponds to the windows with at most 2x memory overhead.\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/models/helio_spectformer.py:48:        checkpoint_layers: list[int] | None = None,\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/models/helio_spectformer.py:75:            checkpoint_layers: indicate which layers to use for checkpointing\n/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/models/helio_spectformer.py:147:            checkpoint_layers=checkpoint_layers,\n/home