            },
            "raw_output": "I validated the diagnosis end-to-end against the installed source and against the T4 itself. Most of it holds. Three things are wrong or incomplete, and one of them changes the recommended fix.\n\n---\n\n# 1. Verification of the diagnosis\n\n## Confirmed\n\n| Claim | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| 65,536 tokens, embed 1280, MLP hidden 5120 | \u2713 | `img_size 4096 / patch 16 = 256`, `256\u00b2 = 65536`; cell 13 output `ts: (1, 13, 1, 4096, 4096)` |\n| `1 \u00d7 65536 \u00d7 5120 \u00d7 4 B = 1.25 GiB` exactly | \u2713 | `335,544,320 \u00d7 4 = 1,342,177,280 B` = 1280 MiB |\n| It is `mlp.fc2`'s input upcast fp32 by PEFT | \u2713 **directly reproduced** | see below |\n| `disable_input_dtype_casting` is a context manager setting `cast_input_dtype_enabled=False` on `BaseTunerLayer`s | \u2713 | `peft/helpers.py:217-252` |\n| `_cast_input_dtype` returns `x` untouched when the flag is False | \u2713 | `peft/tuners/tuners_utils.py:1550-1553` |\n| `HelioSpectformer2D` keeps the full `(1, 65536, 1280)` grid; `HelioSpectformer1D` returns `tokens[:, [0], :]` | \u2713 | `finetune_models.py:304-306`; `helio_spectformer.py:293` |\n| `training.dtype: float32` is inert | \u2713 | `helio_spectformer.py:80`; `dtype` is accepted and dropped |\n| Under `bf16-mixed`, LayerNorm returns fp32 | \u2713 **measured** | even with bf16 weights *and* bf16 input, CUDA autocast returns fp32 |\n| `complex_weight` is 169,082,880 elements = 645 MiB fp32 | \u2713 | measured from `surya.366m.v1.pt` (I said 645 MiB; you said 676 MB \u2014 same number, MiB vs MB) |\n| `AttentionLS.contiguous()` on the `as_strided` views materializes ~1.25 GiB fp32 each | \u2713 | `1\u00d716\u00d7128\u00d7128\u00d74\u00d74\u00d780 = 335,544,320` elements \u2014 *identical element count to the MLP hidden tensor*, so also exactly 1.25 GiB |\n| `builders.py:143-165` hardcodes `pin_memory`/`persistent_workers`, no `prefetch_factor`, shared kwargs | \u2713 | verbatim |\n| `_TRAINING_KEYS` has no `precision`/`accumulate_grad_batches`/`prefetch_factor` | \u2713 | `configs.py:436-439` |\n| `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF` set nowhere | \u2713 | `grep` clean |\n| All budget knobs at floor | \u2713 | except `num_workers` \u2014 see corrections |\n\n**Direct reproduction of the root cause** (real PEFT 0.18.1, `fc1\u2192GELU\u2192fc2` topology, CPU autocast):\n\n```\ncast ENABLED (current): {'in': bfloat16, 'out': float32, 'same_obj': False}\ncast DISABLED (fix):    {'in': bfloat16, 'out': bfloat16, 'same_obj': True}\n```\n\nYour causal chain is exactly right. And note the waste is *doubled*: after PEFT upcasts to fp32, `lora_A` runs under autocast, which immediately allocates a bf16 copy back down. So the fp32 upcast buys nothing at all under mixed precision.\n\n## Correction 1 \u2014 the fp32 token stream comes from `pos_embed`, not LayerNorm\n\nYou attribute the fp32 inter-block token tensor to LayerNorm. LayerNorm *is* fp32 under autocast (I measured it), but LayerNorm's output is never the last op before the residual add \u2014 the residual is `x + mlp(...)`, and `mlp`'s last op is a LoRA `Linear` whose `result.to(torch_result_dtype)` is bf16.\n\nThe real cause is one line, `embedding.py:124`:\n\n```python\nx = x + self.pos_embed      # patch_embed output is bf16 under autocast; pos_embed is a fp32 buffer\n```\n\n`bf16 + fp32 \u2192 fp32` (measured). That promotion at the *entry* to the backbone poisons the residual stream for all 10 blocks, because `fp32 + bf16 \u2192 fp32` at every subsequent block.\n\nThis matters a lot, because it means **casting `pos_embed` alone to half flips all 11 saved/live token tensors from 320 MiB to 160 MiB \u2014 a 1.72 GiB win from one dtype change**, with no `bf16-true` and no optimizer risk. That lever is absent from your ladder.\n\n(`pos_embed` is a *buffer*, so it is not in your 1.43 GB param figure. It is a separate 320 MiB of VRAM: `(1, 65536, 1280)` = 83,886,080 elements.)\n\n## Correction 2 \u2014 `bf16` is the wrong dtype for a T4. Use fp16.\n\nThis is the biggest correction. Measured on this exact GPU:\n\n```\ntorch.cuda.is_bf16_supported()                          -> True\ntorch.cuda.is_bf16_supported(including_emulation=False)  -> False     # emulated on SM75\n\nGEMM at the real MLP shape (16384 \u00d7 1280 \u00d7 5120):\n  float32   56.82 ms\n  bfloat16  90.09 ms     <-- 1.6x SLOWER than fp32\n  float16    9.25 ms     <-- 6.1x FASTER than fp32, 9.7x faster than bf16\n```\n\n`bf16-mixed` on SM75 is emulated: it gets you the memory saving and simultaneously makes every GEMM ~60% slower than plain fp32. T4 has native **fp16** tensor cores (SM75's whole selling point).\n\n`precision=\"16-mixed\"` gives:\n- identical activation memory to `bf16-mixed`\n- ~6\u00d7 faster GEMMs\n- fp32 master weights \u2192 optimizer numerics untouched\n- an automatic `GradScaler` (`lightning/pytorch/plugins/precision/amp.py:53-54`) for the narrower exponent range\n\nAnd fp16 is *more* accurate than bf16 for the weights you'd be rounding (10-bit vs 7-bit mantissa):\n\n```\ncomplex_weight (256,129,1280,2) round-trip relative error:\n  bfloat16  mean 0.00141   max 0.00389\n  float16   mean 0.00018   max 1.00000   # the max is one ~1e-8 value flushed to zero; harmless\n```\n\nAnswering your Q2 sub-question directly: bf16 on SM75 is **correct but emulated and slower than fp32** (`bf16 GEMM rel err 0.0029`, i.e. right). It is not a numerical hazard; it is a throughput hazard.\n\n## Correction 3 \u2014 bf16-true breaks Adam, measurably\n\n`Strategy.setup` (`lightning/pytorch/strategies/strategy.py:154,159`) calls `convert_module` (which is just `module.to(dtype=bfloat16)`, `plugins/precision/half.py:43-44`) **before** `setup_optimizers`. So Adam is constructed over bf16 `Parameter`s, and `exp_avg`/`exp_avg_sq` are allocated in bf16 too. Measured, lr=1e-4, 20 steps, constant unit gradient:\n\n```\nfp32,  p0=0.02 : 0.01800000   (correct)\nbf16,  p0=0.02 : 0.01757812   (2.4% error \u2014 tolerable)\nfp32,  p0=1.0  : 0.99799967   (correct)\nbf16,  p0=1.0  : 1.00000000   <-- ZERO progress. Parameter frozen, silently.\n```\n\nWorse and harder to see: with `beta2=0.999`, the per-step update to `exp_avg_sq` is ~1/1000 of its running value, which is below bf16's 1/256 resolution \u2014 so **`exp_avg_sq` stops updating entirely once warmed up**, and Adam degenerates. Your trainable set here (`lora_A` ~0.014, `lora_B` = 0, head Conv2d w/b ~0.016) happens to sit in the survivable band, but this is a silent-failure mode in a teaching repo, and the loss curve will still look plausible.\n\nVerdict: **do not use `bf16-true`** (or `16-true`). Keep an fp32 master copy, which mixed precision gives you for free.\n\n## Correction 4 \u2014 the `unsqueeze(1).float()` item is a non-issue\n\nNot worth touching, for a reason your diagnosis missed: `FlareMetrics` flattens both sides.\n\n`downstream_apps/sf_triggers/metrics/sf_triggers_metrics.py:65`\n```python\noutput_metrics[\"mse\"] = torch.nn.functional.mse_loss(preds.reshape(-1), target.reshape(-1))\n```\n\nSo there is **no broadcasting, no UserWarning, and no extra copy**: `unsqueeze(1)` is a view, `reshape(-1)` on the contiguous result is a view, and `.float()` on an already-fp32 tensor returns `self`. Under `16-mixed` it costs one 64 MiB fp32 copy of `preds`. Leave it; \"fixing\" it would change nothing observable and would break the shared 1D/2D contract documented at `sf_triggers_metrics.py:21-24`.\n\n## Correction 5 \u2014 `prefetch_factor` is a *host* RAM lever, 0 bytes of VRAM\n\n`ts` is 832 MiB fp32 per sample. With `num_workers=4`, `prefetch_factor=2` (default), `persistent_workers=True`, and the **same `loader_kwargs` reused for val** (`builders.py:143-165`), you can have up to 4\u00d72\u00d72 = 16 batches in flight \u2248 **13 GiB of pinned host memory**. That is almost certainly what produced the four `terminate called without an active exception` lines in cell 44's output. Real bug, worth fixing \u2014 but it will not move `max_memory_allocated` by one byte. Rank it as robustness, not as a fix for this OOM.\n\n## Correction 6 \u2014 minor\n\n- `config_script.yaml` says `num_workers: 8`; the **notebook overrides it to 4** at cell 11. You reported 4 \u2014 right for the notebook, wrong for the YAML. Both matter if you later run `3_finetune_template_1D.py`.\n- `expandable_segments:True` need only precede the **first CUDA allocation**, not the `torch` import (unlike `CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG`, which genuinely must be pre-import). Put it pre-import anyway; harmless and habit-forming.\n- `torch.set_float32_matmul_precision('medium')` (cell 3, and `3_finetune_template_1D.py:261`) is **inert on SM75** \u2014 no TF32 units. Not a bug, just not doing anything.\n- Head/decoder checkpointing (your lever 4) is worth only ~160\u2013320 MiB, not a \"big win\" \u2014 see \u00a75.\n\n## Two observations, both \"do not touch\"\n\n**`spectformer.py:169`** \u2014 when `adaLN=False`:\n```python\nshift_mha, scale_mha, gate_mha, shift_mlp, scale_mlp, gate_mlp = 6 * (1.0,)\n```\nso line 173/177 compute `norm1(x) * 1.0 + 1.0` \u2014 a `+1.0` **shift** where the identity would be `0.0`. This is baked into the pretrained weights, so it is load-bearing. Do not \"fix\" it. Memory-wise it costs one extra `T`-sized allocation per branch per recompute; eliding it would change the math.\n\n**`sf_triggers_metrics.py:37`** \u2014 `self._rrse` is a stateful `torchmetrics` object that is never `reset()`, so the logged `train_metric_rrse` is a run-cumulative average, not a per-step value. Out of scope, worth a follow-up issue.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Per-tensor peak-memory budget\n\nUnits: MiB (2\u00b2\u2070 B). Constants at B=1: `T` = token tensor `(1,65536,1280)`; `M` = MLP hidden `(1,65536,5120)`; `TILE` = `(1,16,128,128,4,4,80)` \u2014 same element count as `M`; `QKV` = `(1,65536,3840)`. Params = 358,701,824 (matches Lightning's printed `1,434.807 MB` and the `706,816` trainable in cell 21).\n\n**Scenarios**\n- **(a)** current: `bf16-mixed`, PEFT cast on, `lora_dropout 0.1`, fp32 params\n- **(b)** `bf16-mixed` + PEFT cast off\n- **(b\u2032)** (b) + `lora_dropout: 0.0`\n- **(c)** `bf16-true`\n- **(d)** **recommended**: `16-mixed` + PEFT cast off + `lora_dropout: 0.0` + frozen params/buffers \u2192 fp16 + the `dual_ln_full` `.to()` fix\n\n### Table A \u2014 held for the entire step\n\n| item | (a) | (b) | (b\u2032) | (c) | (d) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| params | 1368 | 1368 | 1368 | 684 | 684 + 3 |\n| `pos_embed` buffer | 320 | 320 | 320 | 160 | 160 |\n| grads + Adam (0.71 M trainable) | 8 | 8 | 8 | 4 | 8 |\n| `batch[\"ts\"]` + `[\"forecast\"]` | 896 | 896 | 896 | 448 | 896 |\n| 11 \u00d7 saved/live `T` (10 checkpoint frames + current) | 3520 | 3520 | 3520 | 1760 | 1760 |\n| head activations (contiguous copy 320 + conv 64 + PS 64) | 448 | 448 | 448 | 224 | 224 |\n| **held** | **6560** | **6560** | **6560** | **3280** | **3735** |\n| | 6.41 GiB | 6.41 | 6.41 | **3.20** | **3.65** |\n\n### Table B \u2014 added while one `BlockAttention` recomputes, attention half\n\n| item | (a) | (b) | (b\u2032) | (c) | (d) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `qkv` out + base cast-in | 640 | 640 | 640 | 480 | 640 |\n| `qkv` LoRA dropout out + bool mask | 400 | 400 | 0 | 240 | 0 |\n| `q\u00b7scale` | 160 | 160 | 160 | 160 | 160 |\n| `dual_ln_full(k)`, `(v)` \u2014 **fp32 under autocast** | 640 | 640 | 640 | 320 | 320 |\n| `q_tiles` | 160 | 160 | 160 | 160 | 160 |\n| `k_tiles`: fp32 `TILE` + half copy | 1280+640 | 1280+640 | 1280+640 | 640 | 640 |\n| dots + softmax(fp32) | 120 | 120 | 120 | 60 | 60 |\n| `v_tiles` (only one fp32 pair live at a time) | 640 | 640 | 640 | 640 | 640 |\n| `pv` + ungroup + `out` | 480 | 480 | 480 | 480 | 480 |\n| `proj`: **upcast** + dropout + mask + lora cast | 880 | 240 | 160 | 240 | 0 |\n| **attn peak** | **\u22485040** | **\u22484400** | **\u22484080** | **\u22483420** | **\u22483100** |\n| retained after transients free | \u22483100 | \u22482600 | \u22482300 | \u22482200 | \u22481900 |\n\n### Table C \u2014 MLP half, on top of the retained attention set\n\n| item | (a) | (b) | (b\u2032) | (c) | (d) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `norm2` out (fp32 under autocast) | 320 | 320 | 320 | 160 | 320 |\n| `fc1` base cast-in + out | 800 | 800 | 800 | 640 | 800 |\n| `fc1` LoRA dropout out + mask | 400 | 400 | 0 | 240 | 0 |\n| `fc1` `lora_B` out + sum (transient) | 1280 | 1280 | 1280 | 1280 | 1280 |\n| GELU out | 640 | 640 | 640 | 640 | 640 |\n| `fc2` base out | 160 | 160 | 160 | 160 | 160 |\n| **`fc2` `_cast_input_dtype` fp32 copy** | **1280** | **0** | **0** | 0 | **0** |\n| `fc2` LoRA dropout out + mask (`M`-shaped) | 1600 | 960 | 0 | 960 | 0 |\n| `fc2` `lora_A` half cast-in | 640 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| **mlp peak** | **\u22487120** | **\u22484560** | **\u22483200** | **\u22484080** | **\u22483200** |\n\n### Totals \u2014 plain answers\n\n| | peak allocated | fits 14.74 GiB? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **(a) current** | \u2248 6560 + 3100 + 7120 = **16.4 GiB** | **No** \u2014 matches the observed OOM |\n| **(b) bf16-mixed + cast off** | \u2248 6560 + 2600 + 4560 = **13.4 GiB** | **Not reliably.** ~1.3 GiB of headroom against 1.82 GiB of *observed* fragmentation. It removes precisely the allocation that failed, so it may squeak through with `expandable_segments` \u2014 do not plan on it. |\n| **(b\u2032) + `lora_dropout: 0.0`** | \u2248 6560 + 2300 + 3200 = **11.8 GiB** | **Yes, tight** (~3 GiB headroom) |\n| **(c) bf16-true** | \u2248 3280 + 2200 + 4080 = **9.3 GiB** | **Yes on memory. No on numerics** (\u00a7Correction 3) and 1.6\u00d7 slower than fp32 on this GPU |\n| **(d) recommended** | \u2248 3735 + 1900 + 3200 = **8.6 GiB** | **Yes, comfortably** (~6 GiB headroom), fp32 optimizer, ~6\u00d7 faster GEMMs |\n\nMy model over-predicts (a) \u2014 16.4 GiB modelled vs 13.03 GiB observed at the failing allocation \u2014 because several \"peak\" rows free before others allocate, and PyTorch reuses cached blocks. **Treat the tables as an ordered ranking of levers, not as a predictor.** That is what \u00a76's probe is for.\n\n### Levers ranked by measured/derived MiB at peak\n\n| # | lever | \u0394peak | cost | in your ladder? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | PEFT `cast_input_dtype_enabled = False` | \u22122.5 to \u22123.1 GiB | zero \u2014 the fp32 copy was immediately re-cast to bf16 anyway | yes (#2) |\n| 2 | **`lora_dropout: 0.0` (one YAML character)** | **\u22121.4 to \u22122.2 GiB** | negligible regularization at r=2 with 8 samples | **no \u2014 missed** |\n| 3 | **Frozen params + buffers \u2192 fp16** (incl. `pos_embed`) | **\u22122.5 GiB** | 0.018% weight rounding | **partially (#3), via the wrong mechanism** |\n| 4 | **`precision=\"16-mixed\"` instead of `bf16-mixed`** | 0 (same memory) | **+6\u00d7 GEMM speed** | **no \u2014 missed** |\n| 5 | `dual_ln_full(...).to(k.dtype)` in `AttentionLS` | \u22121.3 GiB | vendored edit, 0.05% rounding | no |\n| 6 | `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True` | recovers the observed 1.82 GiB reserved-unallocated | zero | yes (#1) |\n| 7 | `num_sanity_val_steps=0` | ~0.3 GiB of fragmentation + 2 wasted 832 MiB H2D copies | zero | yes (#5) |\n| 8 | checkpoint `head_unembed` | \u2212160 to \u2212320 MiB | recompute cost | yes (#4) \u2014 overrated |\n| 9 | `prefetch_factor` / worker count | **0 VRAM**, \u22126 to \u221211 GiB *host* RAM | zero | yes, mis-attributed |\n| 10 | resolution reduction | large | destroys pretrained `complex_weight` fidelity | yes (#6) \u2014 **not needed** |\n\nLevers 1\u20134 + 6 land you at ~8.6 GiB. **Lever 10 is off the table, and so is `bf16-true`.**\n\n---\n\n# 3. Persistently disabling the PEFT input-dtype cast\n\nAdd to `workshop_infrastructure/utils.py`, immediately after `apply_peft_lora` (after line 380):\n\n```python\ndef disable_peft_input_dtype_cast(model: torch.nn.Module) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Stop PEFT from upcasting activations to the adapter weight dtype.\n\n    ``peft.tuners.lora.Linear.forward`` calls ``_cast_input_dtype(x,\n    lora_A.weight.dtype)`` before the adapter branch.  Under mixed precision the\n    adapter weights are fp32 while ``x`` is fp16/bf16, so PEFT allocates a fresh\n    fp32 copy of the activation -- and autocast then casts it straight back down\n    for ``lora_A``.  The copy buys nothing and is charged at the widest point in\n    the network: for HelioSpectformer2D at 4096x4096, ``mlp.fc2``'s input is\n    (1, 65536, 5120), so the fp32 copy is exactly 1.25 GiB, per block, on every\n    gradient-checkpoint recompute.\n\n    ``peft.helpers.disable_input_dtype_casting`` does the same thing but as a\n    context manager, which cannot wrap ``trainer.fit()`` cleanly.  This sets the\n    flag once, permanently, and is a no-op on a model without adapters.\n\n    Only safe under autocast (``precision`` ending in ``-mixed``): autocast is\n    what reconciles the fp32 adapter weight with the half-precision input.  With\n    ``*-true`` precision the adapter weights are already half, ``x.dtype ==\n    dtype``, and PEFT skips the copy on its own -- so this becomes redundant\n    rather than wrong.\n\n    Returns:\n        The number of tuner layers touched, so a silently-ineffective call is\n        visible in the log.\n    \"\"\"\n    from peft.tuners.tuners_utils import BaseTunerLayer\n\n    touched = 0\n    for module in model.modules():\n        if isinstance(module, BaseTunerLayer):\n            module.cast_input_dtype_enabled = False\n            touched += 1\n    print(f\"[LoRA] Disabled PEFT input-dtype casting on {touched} tuner layer(s).\")\n    return touched\n```\n\nVerified against the installed PEFT: `cast_input_dtype_enabled` is a plain instance attribute set in `LoraLayer.__init__` (`peft/tuners/lora/layer.py:121`), and `_cast_input_dtype` reads it via `getattr(..., True)` (`tuners_utils.py:1550`) \u2014 so setting it on the wrapper layer is the whole mechanism. My reproduction above confirms it returns the *same object* (`same_obj: True`), i.e. zero allocation.\n\n**Design choice \u2014 keep it a separate function, do not fold it into `apply_peft_lora`.** `apply_peft_lora` should not silently depend on the precision the `Trainer` will later be built with. Two levers, two call sites, each visible. Per CLAUDE.md's clarity directive.\n\n**Should it be gated on precision?** No. Under `*-true` it is a harmless no-op (dtypes already match), and hard-coding a precision check inside `utils.py` couples it to the Trainer. Document the condition in the docstring (done) and call it unconditionally.\n\n**Do both `cast off` and half-precision weights belong in the plan?** Yes \u2014 they solve different halves. Disabling the cast fixes *activations* (Tables B/C). Casting frozen weights fixes *parameters and the residual stream* (Table A). Under the recommended (d) they compose: frozen weights fp16, trainable adapters fp32, autocast reconciles, PEFT does not interfere. Only under `bf16-true`/`16-true` does the cast-disable become redundant \u2014 and (d) does not use those.\n\n## Companion helper: cast the frozen half to fp16\n\nAlso in `workshop_infrastructure/utils.py`:\n\n```python\ndef cast_frozen_to(model: torch.nn.Module, dtype: torch.dtype) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"Cast every frozen parameter and every float buffer to ``dtype``.\n\n    Trainable parameters are left in fp32 so the optimizer keeps a full-precision\n    master copy.  Under autocast the two dtypes coexist: autocast casts the fp32\n    adapter weights down for each matmul, and the frozen half-precision weights\n    are already at the compute dtype.\n\n    Two things this buys on HelioSpectformer2D at 4096x4096, beyond the obvious\n    halving of the 1.37 GiB of frozen weights:\n\n      * ``LinearEmbedding.pos_embed`` is a (1, 65536, 1280) *buffer*, not a\n        parameter -- 320 MiB in fp32.  ``embedding.py:124`` adds it to the\n        half-precision patch-embedding output, and fp32 + half promotes to fp32,\n        which pins the entire inter-block residual stream to fp32 for all 10\n        blocks.  With all 10 layers gradient-checkpointed that is 11 live\n        (1, 65536, 1280) tensors: 3.44 GiB in fp32 versus 1.72 GiB in half.\n      * ``complex_weight`` (169 M elements, 645 MiB fp32) halves.  It is upcast\n        back to fp32 inside ``SpectralGatingNetwork.forward`` for the FFT, so the\n        rounding is permanent; measured relative error is 0.018% mean in fp16\n        (0.14% in bf16).  That is below the activation noise floor of mixed\n        precision, but it *is* a change to a pretrained tensor -- say so in the\n        run log rather than hiding it.\n\n    Call this *after* ``load_pretrained_weights()`` and ``apply_peft_lora()``, so\n    the checkpoint is read at full precision and rounded exactly once, and so\n    ``requires_grad`` already reflects the chosen fine-tuning regime.  Under full\n    fine-tuning (``use_lora: false, freeze_backbone: false``) nothing is frozen\n    and this is a no-op.\n\n    Returns:\n        ``(n_params_cast, n_buffers_cast)``.\n    \"\"\"\n    n_params = 0\n    for param in model.parameters():\n        if not param.requires_grad and param.is_floating_point() and param.dtype != dtype:\n            param.data = param.data.to(dtype)\n            n_params += 1\n\n    n_buffers = 0\n    for buf in model.buffers():\n        if buf.is_floating_point() and buf.dtype != dtype:\n            buf.data = buf.data.to(dtype)\n            n_buffers += 1\n\n    print(f\"[PRECISION] Cast {n_params} frozen parameter(s) and {n_buffers} buffer(s) to {dtype}.\")\n    return n_params, n_buffers\n```\n\n**Safety audit** \u2014 every op that sees a frozen half-precision weight, under autocast:\n\n| op | site | verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Conv2d` patch embed | `embedding.py:35` | autocast bf16/fp16 list \u2713 (measured) |\n| `x + pos_embed` | `embedding.py:124` | half + half \u2192 half \u2713 (this is the point) |\n| `LayerNorm` (`norm1/norm2/dual_ln_*`) | throughout | autocast promotes weight to fp32, returns fp32 \u2713 measured with half weights, no error |\n| `complex_weight.to(torch.float32)` \u2192 `view_as_complex` | `spectformer.py:65-67` | \u2713 works; costs a 645 MiB transient per spectral recompute (bounded by the larger attention peak) |\n| `torch.fft.rfft2` | `spectformer.py:62` | never sees half \u2014 `x.to(torch.float32)` at line 61 precedes it. Direct half input **raises `RuntimeError: Unsupported dtype`** (I confirmed for bf16) |\n| `Linear` (`qkv`, `proj`, `to_dynamic_projection`, `fc1`, `fc2`) | throughout | autocast half list \u2713 |\n| `F.pad`, `as_strided`, `matmul`, `softmax`, `chunk` | `transformer_ls.py` | \u2713 |\n| `Conv2d` + `PixelShuffle` head | `embedding.py:145-152` | head is **trainable** \u2192 stays fp32, autocast handles it. Direct half also works: measured `fp16 Conv2d+PixelShuffle -> float16 (1,1,4096,4096)` \u2713 |\n| `DropPath` | `spectformer.py:95` | `nn.Identity` (drop_path_rate=0) \u2713 |\n\n**One trap for the notebook**: after `cast_frozen_to`, a *bare* forward with no autocast (cell 23's smoke test) hits `Conv2d(fp16 weight, fp32 input)` \u2192 `RuntimeError`. Either place the cast after cell 23, or wrap the probe in `torch.autocast`. \u00a76 and \u00a78 handle this.\n\n## Answering the rest of Q2\n\n- **Does `bf16-true` reliably cast a `PeftModel`?** Yes. `HalfPrecision.convert_module` is literally `module.to(dtype=...)` (`half.py:43-44`), which walks every parameter and buffer \u2014 `lora_A`, `lora_B`, and both branches of the `ModulesToSaveWrapper` around `head_unembed`. Nothing is missed.\n- **Do `discover_head_modules` / `apply_peft_lora` still hold?** Yes, unchanged and unaffected. Both run in the notebook before the `Trainer` exists; `convert_module` is a pure dtype change that preserves module structure, qualified names, and `requires_grad`. `tests/test_lora_setup.py` stays valid. The `.endswith` collision check and the `head_` convention are untouched.\n- **Does Adam make progress on bf16 params at lr 1e-4?** Partially and unreliably \u2014 see Correction 3. You need an fp32 master copy. (d) gives you one.\n- **`torch.fft.rfft2` with bf16 `complex_weight`?** It never sees bf16 (line 61/66 upcast first), so it runs. The accuracy cost is the *weight rounding*, not the FFT: 0.141% mean / 0.389% max relative error in bf16, 0.018% mean in fp16. Upcasting back to fp32 does not recover it. Secondary cost: the upcast allocates a fresh 645 MiB fp32 tensor on every forward *and* every recompute of the 2 spectral blocks, where fp32 storage made `.to()` a free no-op.\n- **`PixelShuffle` in bf16?** Yes \u2014 measured, both under autocast and with half weights directly.\n- **`bf16-true` safe on SM75?** Numerically yes, performance no. See Correction 2.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Head/decoder gradient checkpointing\n\nHonest sizing first: `LinearDecoder.forward` (`embedding.py:154-172`) does `rearrange(x, \"B (H W) D -> B D H W\")`, which is a reshape+permute \u2192 non-contiguous, so `Conv2d` forces a contiguous copy of a `T`-sized tensor, and that copy is saved (the head *is* trainable). Plus conv out 64 MiB + PixelShuffle out 64 MiB. So checkpointing buys **~320 MiB in fp32, ~160 MiB in fp16** \u2014 the smallest of the real levers, and it costs a full re-run of the decoder on every backward. Implement it behind a flag, do not enable it by default.\n\n`workshop_infrastructure/models/finetune_models.py`, replacing lines 304-306:\n\n```python\n    def forward(self, batch):\n        tokens = self.backbone.forward(batch)\n        if self.checkpoint_head and self.training and torch.is_grad_enabled():\n            # The decoder's saved activations are modest next to a backbone block\n            # (one (B, L, D)-sized contiguous copy that einops' reshape+permute\n            # forces Conv2d to make, plus two (B, 1, H, W) tensors) -- ~320 MiB in\n            # fp32, ~160 MiB in half at 4096x4096.  Off by default: the recompute\n            # is a poor trade unless you are within that margin of fitting.\n            #\n            # use_reentrant=False matches the backbone's checkpoint calls in\n            # spectformer.py:292 and helio_spectformer.py:288.  Do not mix the two\n            # flavours: the reentrant variant does not tolerate a checkpointed\n            # region whose inputs do not require grad, which is exactly the case\n            # here under a frozen backbone.\n            return checkpoint(self.head_unembed, tokens, use_reentrant=False)\n        return self.head_unembed(tokens)  # (B, L, D) -> (B, C, H, W)\n```\n\nConstructor: add the parameter after line 261 (`ft_out_chans: int = 1,`):\n\n```python\n        ft_checkpoint_head: bool = False,\n```\nand after the `head_unembed` construction (after line 302):\n```python\n        self.checkpoint_head = ft_checkpoint_head\n```\n\nImport at the top of `finetune_models.py`:\n```python\nfrom torch.utils.checkpoint import checkpoint\n```\n\n**Gating.** Yes, gate it \u2014 but on a **constructor argument passed through `from_config(**overrides)`**, not on a new `ModelConfig` field. `ft_out_chans` and `ft_unembedding_type` already work that way (`finetune_models.py:308-335` explicitly documents overrides as"
          },
          {
            "title": "python3 -c \" import json p='/home/jovyan/.claude/projects/-home-jovyan-adi-wdir-surya-workshop-workshop-infrastructure-models/040f0722-75b9-436b-8750-58322a398656/tool-results/toolu_bdrk_01HnuZJWvAGkjV2hNtssgRTw.json' d=json.load(open(p)) t=''.join(b.get('text','') for b in d) print(t[26000:52000]) \"",
            "raw_output": "the channel for non-`ModelConfig` fields), and `checkpoint_layers` in `ModelConfig` is about the backbone. Keeping the head's knob out of `ModelConfig` also keeps `tests/test_model_config.py` and `ModelConfig.__post_init__`'s `checkpoint_layers`-vs-`depth` invariant untouched.\n\n**The two checkpoint loops.** `spectformer.py:288-296` (`SpectFormer.forward`) and `helio_spectformer.py:279-291` (`HelioSpectFormer.forward_with_cls_token`) are independent transcriptions of the same loop over `chain(blocks_spectral_gating, blocks_attention)` gated on `self.backbone._checkpoint_layers`. Both call `checkpoint(blk, tokens, noise, use_reentrant=False)`.\n\nThe change above touches **neither** \u2014 `HelioSpectformer2D` routes through `HelioSpectFormer.forward` \u2192 `SpectFormer.forward` (`spectformer.py:288-296`); `forward_with_cls_token` is the 1D/CLS path only. Note for the record: the 1D path has no decoder to checkpoint (`helio_spectformer.py:293` returns a single token), so `ft_checkpoint_head` has no 1D analogue and `HelioSpectformer1D` needs no mirror change. If you later change how the backbone loop checkpoints, both loops must move together.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Config plumbing\n\n## 5a. `precision` on `TrainingConfig`\n\nFour edits in `workshop_infrastructure/configs.py`, all required \u2014 the third is the one people forget, and CLAUDE.md's \"unknown keys raise\" property means a YAML key without a dataclass field is a hard error, not a no-op.\n\n**(i)** After `VALID_DETERMINISTIC` (line 52):\n\n```python\n# Passed straight to lightning.Trainer(precision=...).  Restricted to the four values\n# that make sense for this model:\n#   \"32-true\"    -- no autocast.  Only fits at reduced resolution.\n#   \"16-mixed\"   -- fp16 autocast, fp32 master weights + GradScaler.  The right default\n#                   on any pre-Ampere GPU: SM75 (T4) has native fp16 tensor cores but\n#                   emulates bf16, where a 16384x1280x5120 GEMM measures 9.3 ms in fp16,\n#                   56.8 ms in fp32 and 90.1 ms in bf16.\n#   \"bf16-mixed\" -- bf16 autocast.  Prefer on Ampere+ (SM80+), where bf16 is native and\n#                   its wider exponent range removes the need for a GradScaler.\n#   \"bf16-true\"  -- bf16 weights.  NOT recommended: lightning casts the module before\n#                   building the optimizer (Strategy.setup), so Adam's exp_avg_sq lives\n#                   in bf16, where the beta2=0.999 update is below the 2^-8 mantissa\n#                   resolution and stops accumulating.  Measured: a parameter of\n#                   magnitude 1.0 at lr 1e-4 does not move at all over 20 steps.\nVALID_PRECISION = (\"32-true\", \"16-mixed\", \"bf16-mixed\", \"bf16-true\")\n```\n\n**(ii)** In the `TrainingConfig` dataclass, after line 254 (`dtype: str = \"float32\"`):\n\n```python\n    # One of VALID_PRECISION (above).  Note this is the ONLY precision control that does\n    # anything: `dtype` above is accepted for backwards compatibility and dropped on the\n    # floor (helio_spectformer.py:80 documents it as unused).\n    precision: str = \"16-mixed\"\n    # Trades wall time for memory: N micro-batches are accumulated before each optimizer\n    # step, giving an effective batch of batch_size * N at the memory cost of batch_size.\n    # Already at batch_size: 1 for HelioSpectformer2D at 4096x4096, so this is the only\n    # way left to raise the effective batch.\n    accumulate_grad_batches: int = 1\n```\n\n**(iii)** In the `TrainingConfig(...)` construction, after line 428 (`dtype=training.get(\"dtype\", \"float32\"),`):\n\n```python\n        precision=training.get(\"precision\", \"16-mixed\"),\n        accumulate_grad_batches=training.get(\"accumulate_grad_batches\", 1),\n```\n\n**(iv)** In `_TRAINING_KEYS` (lines 436-439) \u2014 add both to the frozenset:\n\n```python\n_TRAINING_KEYS = frozenset({\n    \"learning_rate\", \"max_epochs\", \"batch_size\", \"num_workers\", \"seed\", \"deterministic\",\n    \"rollout_steps\", \"drop_hmi_probability\", \"use_latitude_in_learned_flow\", \"dtype\",\n    \"precision\", \"accumulate_grad_batches\",\n})\n```\n\n**(v)** `__post_init__` validation \u2014 insert into `TrainingConfig.__post_init__` after the `seed` check (i.e. after line 274), following the existing style of `deterministic`'s check:\n\n```python\n        if self.precision not in VALID_PRECISION:\n            raise ValueError(\n                f\"Unknown training.precision {self.precision!r}. \"\n                f\"Valid values are: {', '.join(VALID_PRECISION)}.\"\n            )\n        if not isinstance(self.accumulate_grad_batches, int) or isinstance(\n            self.accumulate_grad_batches, bool\n        ) or self.accumulate_grad_batches < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\n                \"training.accumulate_grad_batches must be an integer >= 1, got \"\n                f\"{self.accumulate_grad_batches!r}.\"\n            )\n```\n\n**Should `precision`'s default change from `bf16-mixed` to `16-mixed`?** Yes, and say why in the comment (done in (i)). This repo's users are on shared T4s. But note `16-mixed` is not free: a `GradScaler` can skip steps on non-finite gradients, and with `max_samples: 10` a couple of skipped steps is a large fraction of the run. Log it \u2014 Lightning surfaces scaler state, and a run where the loss is flat with `grad_scale` collapsing is the signature.\n\n**No cross-field invariant to add** beyond (v): unlike `deterministic` \u00d7 `learned_flow`, `precision` has no incompatible partner in this config.\n\n**`build_trainer()`** \u2014 `downstream_apps/sf_triggers/3_finetune_template_1D.py:241` and `downstream_apps/template/3_finetune_template_1D.py:238`, identically:\n\n```python\n        # Precision is a config value, not a constant: the right choice is GPU-dependent\n        # (fp16 on SM75, bf16 on SM80+) and it is the primary memory lever for the 2D head.\n        # The CPU fallback ignores it -- neither autocast dtype has a fast CPU path.\n        precision=cfg.precision if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"32-true\",\n```\nand add to the same `L.Trainer(...)` call:\n```python\n        accumulate_grad_batches=cfg.accumulate_grad_batches,\n        num_sanity_val_steps=0,\n```\n\n**Test.** Add to `tests/test_model_config.py`: a valid `precision` round-trips; an invalid one raises naming the alternatives; an unknown `training:` key still raises (guards against someone adding to `_TRAINING_KEYS` without the field, and vice versa).\n\n**YAML** \u2014 `downstream_apps/sf_triggers/configs/config_script.yaml`, in the `training:` block near `dtype:` (line ~\"dtype: float32\"):\n\n```yaml\n  # Mixed-precision mode, passed to lightning.Trainer. This is the real precision control;\n  # `dtype` below is inert (the backbone documents it as unused).\n  #\n  #   16-mixed    fp16 autocast + fp32 master weights + GradScaler. Correct for T4/V100\n  #               (SM70/75): native fp16 tensor cores. Measured on a T4 at this model's\n  #               MLP GEMM shape: 9.3 ms fp16 vs 56.8 ms fp32 vs 90.1 ms bf16 (bf16 is\n  #               EMULATED below SM80 -- slower than fp32, not faster).\n  #   bf16-mixed  Prefer on A100/H100 (SM80+): native, and no GradScaler needed.\n  #   bf16-true   Do not use. Lightning casts the module before building the optimizer,\n  #               so Adam's second moment lives in bf16 and stops accumulating.\n  precision: 16-mixed\n\n  # Effective batch = batch_size * accumulate_grad_batches, at the memory cost of\n  # batch_size alone. batch_size is pinned at 1 for the 2D head at 4096x4096, so this is\n  # the only remaining way to raise it.\n  accumulate_grad_batches: 1\n```\n\n## 5b. `prefetch_factor` in `builders.py`\n\nThree problems in `builders.py:143-165`, one signature change. Note again: this is **host** memory, ~0 VRAM.\n\n```python\ndef build_helio_dataloaders(\n    cfg,\n    dataset_cls: Type[Dataset],\n    scalers: Any = None,\n    num_workers: int | None = None,\n    seed: int | None = None,\n    prefetch_factor: int | None = None,\n    **task_kwargs,\n) -> Tuple[DataLoader, DataLoader]:\n```\n\nDocstring addition:\n\n```\n        prefetch_factor: Batches each worker keeps queued ahead of the training loop.\n            ``None`` uses PyTorch's default of 2. This is a *host* memory knob, not a GPU\n            one: one Surya sample is (13, T, 4096, 4096) fp32 = 832 MiB, so a train loader\n            with 4 workers and prefetch_factor 2 can hold 6.5 GiB of pinned host RAM --\n            and because ``persistent_workers=True`` keeps the validation workers alive\n            through training, the two loaders together can hold twice that. On a machine\n            where that overcommits, workers die with \"terminate called without an active\n            exception\" and the run fails for a reason that looks nothing like its cause.\n            Set 1 there. It will not change ``torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()``.\n```\n\nReplacing lines 143-165:\n\n```python\n    loader_kwargs = dict(\n        batch_size=cfg.batch_size,\n        num_workers=workers,\n        pin_memory=True,\n        drop_last=True,\n    )\n    if workers > 0:\n        # \"spawn\": the dataset holds an s3fs/boto3 handle that does not survive fork.\n        # Both of these are rejected outright when num_workers == 0.\n        loader_kwargs[\"multiprocessing_context\"] = \"spawn\"\n        loader_kwargs[\"persistent_workers\"] = True\n        loader_kwargs[\"worker_init_fn\"] = partial(_seed_worker, base_seed=base_seed)\n        # DataLoader rejects prefetch_factor when num_workers == 0, so it is set here\n        # rather than above, and only when the caller asked for a non-default value.\n        if prefetch_factor is not None:\n            loader_kwargs[\"prefetch_factor\"] = prefetch_factor\n\n    shuffle_generator = torch.Generator()\n    shuffle_generator.manual_seed(base_seed)\n\n    train_loader = DataLoader(\n        train_dataset, shuffle=True, generator=shuffle_generator, **loader_kwargs\n    )\n    # No generator for validation: it is not shuffled, so there is nothing to seed.\n    val_loader = DataLoader(val_dataset, shuffle=False, **loader_kwargs)\n    return train_loader, val_loader\n```\n\n**Do NOT add `prefetch_factor` to `TrainingConfig`.** It is a per-machine host-RAM knob, exactly like `s3_cache_dir` and the `num_workers` notebook override, and `build_helio_dataloaders` already takes `num_workers`/`seed` as call-site overrides for precisely this class of setting (`builders.py:114,124-130`). Adding it to the YAML would put a machine-specific value in a checked-in shared file \u2014 the thing the config header explicitly warns against.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Memory-probe cell\n\nInsert as a **new code cell at JSON index 24**, i.e. immediately *after* the existing forward-pass smoke test at index 23 and *before* the \"Define your metrics\" markdown (currently index 24). Rationale: cell 23 has already proven shapes are right on CPU, `output`/`batch` are live in the namespace, and cells 28/30/32 consume `output` \u2014 so the probe must not disturb either. Everything below is scoped to a temporary GPU copy and torn down.\n\nThe probe must also carry a preceding markdown cell (index 24, pushing the probe to 25) so the notebook reads as a lesson rather than a debugging scratchpad \u2014 CLAUDE.md's secondary objective.\n\n```python\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Measure, don't guess: one real forward+backward, peak GPU memory reported.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Lightning reports \"1,434.807 MB total estimated model params size\" and stops there.\n# That is the *static* cost. What OOMs a 15 GB T4 is the transient cost of one\n# gradient-checkpoint recompute, which nothing in the training log shows you.\n#\n# Run this once per lever you change, and record the number. Each line below is a\n# lever; comment them in one at a time and watch `peak` move. Guessing which lever\n# matters is how people end up reducing resolution for no reason.\n#\n# Every allocation is torn down at the end, so this leaves the GPU as it found it and\n# `trainer.fit()` below starts from a clean pool.\nimport gc\nimport torch\n\nPROBE_PRECISION = torch.float16   # match training.precision: 16-mixed on a T4 (SM75:\n                                  # native fp16 tensor cores, bf16 is EMULATED and slower\n                                  # than fp32). Use torch.bfloat16 on SM80+.\n\ndef probe_step_memory(model, batch, autocast_dtype=PROBE_PRECISION, label=\"\"):\n    \"\"\"Run one forward+backward on GPU and report peak allocated/reserved memory.\"\"\"\n    dev = torch.device(\"cuda\")\n    model = model.to(dev)\n    gpu_batch = {k: (v.to(dev, non_blocking=True) if torch.is_tensor(v) else v)\n                 for k, v in batch.items()}\n\n    model.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\n    gc.collect()\n    torch.cuda.empty_cache()\n    torch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()\n    base = torch.cuda.memory_allocated()\n\n    # autocast is not optional here: with a half-precision frozen backbone and fp32\n    # adapters, autocast is what reconciles the two dtypes. Without it you get\n    # \"expected scalar type Half but found Float\" from the patch-embedding Conv2d.\n    with torch.autocast(\"cuda\", dtype=autocast_dtype):\n        out = model(gpu_batch)\n        target = gpu_batch[\"forecast\"]\n        loss = torch.nn.functional.mse_loss(out.reshape(-1), target.reshape(-1).float())\n    loss.backward()\n\n    peak_alloc = torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() / 2**30\n    peak_resv = torch.cuda.max_memory_reserved() / 2**30\n    total = torch.cuda.get_device_properties(0).total_memory / 2**30\n    frag = peak_resv - peak_alloc\n    print(f\"{label or 'probe'}:\")\n    print(f\"  static (model on GPU, pre-step) : {base / 2**30:6.2f} GiB\")\n    print(f\"  peak allocated                  : {peak_alloc:6.2f} GiB\")\n    print(f\"  peak reserved                   : {peak_resv:6.2f} GiB\")\n    print(f\"  reserved-but-unallocated (frag) : {frag:6.2f} GiB\")\n    print(f\"  device total                    : {total:6.2f} GiB \"\n          f\"-> headroom {total - peak_resv:5.2f} GiB\")\n    print(f\"  loss                            : {loss.item():.6f}  ({out.dtype})\")\n\n    del out, loss, target, gpu_batch\n    model.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\n    model.to(\"cpu\")\n    gc.collect()\n    torch.cuda.empty_cache()\n    return peak_alloc\n\n_ = probe_step_memory(model, batch, label=\"baseline\")\n```\n\nTwo notes on using it:\n- It must be run **after** whichever levers you are measuring have been applied to `model` (i.e. after `disable_peft_input_dtype_cast` / `cast_frozen_to`), which is why the ordering in \u00a78 puts those before it.\n- `model.to(\"cpu\")` at the end matters. Leaving 1.4 GB of weights resident and then letting Lightning move them again is a reliable way to make the first real training step OOM for reasons unrelated to the model.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Ordering and stopping rule\n\nRestart the kernel first \u2014 non-negotiable. 12,383 MiB is still held by PID 662721; I re-confirmed this just now (`nvidia-smi`: 2713 MiB free). No measurement before the restart means anything.\n\n### Phase 0 \u2014 prerequisites (do all, then measure once)\n\n| step | change | why |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0.1 | Restart the kernel | reclaim 12.4 GiB |\n| 0.2 | `os.environ.setdefault(\"PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF\", \"expandable_segments:True\")` in cell 3 | recovers the 1.82 GiB observed reserved-but-unallocated. Free. |\n| 0.3 | Run cells 2\u219223, then the probe | establishes the baseline number |\n\nExpect the baseline probe to OOM or land near ~13 GiB. That is the datum.\n\n### Phase 1 \u2014 strictly necessary (three changes, ~5.5 GiB)\n\n| step | change | expected \u0394 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1.1 | `disable_peft_input_dtype_cast(model)` after `apply_peft_lora` | \u22122.5 to \u22123.1 GiB |\n| 1.2 | `lora_dropout: 0.0` in the YAML | \u22121.4 to \u22122.2 GiB |\n| 1.3 | `precision: 16-mixed` (config + Trainer cell) | 0 GiB, ~6\u00d7 faster |\n\nRe-probe. Expected ~11\u201312 GiB peak. **This alone should fit.** If it does, you can stop here \u2014 but do 2.1 anyway, it is free memory *and* free speed.\n\n### Phase 2 \u2014 recommended (one change, ~2.5 GiB)\n\n| step | change | expected \u0394 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2.1 | `cast_frozen_to(model, torch.float16)` after 1.1 | \u22122.5 GiB (params 0.67 + `pos_embed` 0.16 + residual stream 1.72) |\n\nRe-probe. Expected **~8.5\u20139 GiB, ~6 GiB headroom**. Stop here. Run training. Confirm the loss decreases over the first epoch and that `head_unembed`'s weights actually move (it is fp32, so they will).\n\n### Phase 3 \u2014 only if Phase 2 still does not fit (it will)\n\n| step | change | expected \u0394 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3.1 | `.to(k.dtype)` on the two `dual_ln_full` calls, `transformer_ls.py:105-106` | \u22121.3 GiB. Vendored edit \u2014 record it, CLAUDE.md warns there is no diff signal against upstream |\n| 3.2 | `ft_checkpoint_head=True` | \u2212160 MiB |\n| 3.3 | `accumulate_grad_batches: 2` with `batch_size: 1` | 0 GiB of peak; raises the effective batch, which is the actual scientific problem with 8 training samples |\n\n### Explicitly NOT in the plan\n\n- **`bf16-true` / `16-true`** \u2014 silently breaks Adam (measured). If someone insists, they must verify parameters actually move, not just that the loss curve looks alive.\n- **Resolution reduction / interpolating `complex_weight` off its (256, 129) grid** \u2014 unnecessary. Phase 2 leaves 6 GiB of headroom. Do not spend pretrained-weight fidelity you do not have to.\n- **`prefetch_factor` / `num_workers`** as a VRAM fix \u2014 it is not one. Fix it separately, as host-memory robustness (\u00a75b), and do not credit it with any GiB.\n\n### If Phase 2 still OOMs\n\nThen one of my dtype assumptions is wrong for your build, and the probe will say which. Read `torch.cuda.memory_summary()` at the failure and check, in order:\n1. `peak reserved \u2212 peak allocated` > 2 GiB \u2192 `expandable_segments` did not take effect (set before the first CUDA op, and confirm with `torch.cuda.memory.get_allocator_backend()`).\n2. `static (model on GPU, pre-step)` > 1.0 GiB \u2192 `cast_frozen_to` did not fire. Check its printed count; under full fine-tuning nothing is frozen and it is a no-op by design.\n3. Peak still shows a 1.25 GiB step \u2192 the PEFT cast is still on. `disable_peft_input_dtype_cast` prints the layer count; if it says 0, PEFT changed its class hierarchy.\n4. All three clean and still OOM \u2192 the residual stream is still fp32. Print `tokens.dtype` inside `SpectFormer.forward` (`spectformer.py:288`). If it is fp32, something else is promoting it, and `pos_embed` is the first place to look.\n\nOnly after all four come back clean is resolution reduction on the table.\n\n---\n\n# 8. Cell-index-accurate edit list\n\nRe-read from the working tree just now (the notebook is `M` in `git status`, so these are the live indices, 46 cells, `trainer.fit` at index 44 / `exec_count 25` \u2014 matching your report).\n\nEdits are listed **in ascending index order using pre-edit indices**; insertions shift everything after them, so apply from the bottom up or track the offsets noted.\n\n| idx | type | action |\n|---|---|---|\n| **3** | code (`exec 4`) | **Edit.** Add one line inside the existing pre-import block, directly under `os.environ.setdefault(\"CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG\", \":4096:8\")`: `os.environ.setdefault(\"PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF\", \"expandable_segments:True\")`. Extend the existing comment: unlike `CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG` this one only has to precede the first CUDA *allocation*, but it lives here for the same reason and to keep one \"set before torch does anything\" block. Also import the two new helpers: `from workshop_infrastructure.utils import disable_peft_input_dtype_cast, cast_frozen_to`. **Do not** move or reorder anything else in this cell. |\n| **11** | code (`exec 8`) | **Edit.** Add `prefetch_factor=1,` next to `num_workers=4,`, with a one-line comment: one sample is 832 MiB fp32, and `persistent_workers=True` keeps the val workers resident through training \u2014 this is what the four `terminate called without an active exception` lines in the old cell-44 output were. Note in the comment that it does **not** affect GPU memory. |\n| **20** | markdown | **Edit.** Two corrections. (a) It says `apply_peft_lora()` \"re-enables gradients for every parameter outside `backbone.*`\" \u2014 that is not what the code does. `apply_peft_lora` (`utils.py:323,337`) passes `discover_head_modules(model)` to PEFT as `modules_to_save`; the `head_` prefix convention *is* the mechanism, as CLAUDE.md states. The template's own copy of this markdown is correct; this one drifted. Fix it. (b) Append a paragraph on the two precision levers about to be applied, and why the fp32 upcast exists at all (PEFT aligning activations to fp32 adapter weights) \u2014 this is the teachable moment of the whole exercise. |\n| **21** | code (`exec 13`) | **Edit.** After `model = apply_peft_lora(model, cfg.model.lora_config)`, add the two calls, inside the `if cfg.model.use_lora:` block for the first and outside it for the second:<br>`disable_peft_input_dtype_cast(model)` \u2014 with a comment noting this is only correct under `*-mixed` precision and is a harmless no-op under `*-true`.<br>`cast_frozen_to(model, torch.float16)` \u2014 with a comment that it must follow `load_pretrained_weights` (cell 19) so the checkpoint is read at full precision and rounded once, and that `torch.bfloat16` is the right argument on SM80+. |\n| **22** | markdown | **Edit.** Add one sentence: from here on the model's frozen half is fp16, so any bare `model.forward(...)` must be wrapped in `torch.autocast` \u2014 a raw call now raises `expected scalar type Half but found Float` from the patch-embedding Conv2d. This is the trap most likely to waste someone's afternoon. |\n| **23** | code (`exec 14`) | **Edit \u2014 required, not optional.** After cell 21's `cast_frozen_to`, this cell breaks. Wrap the forward:<br>`with torch.autocast(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\", dtype=torch.float16):`<br>`    output = model.forward(batch)`<br>Two further notes for the comment: this still runs on **CPU** (nothing has been moved to GPU yet), and `output` deliberately retains its autograd graph, which cells 28/30/32 depend on \u2014 do not add `torch.no_grad()`. |\n| **24** | markdown | **INSERT** before the current index 24. Short lesson: why a static parameter count does not predict an OOM, and why the peak lives inside a gradient-checkpoint recompute. Everything from here shifts +1. |\n| **25** | code | **INSERT** the \u00a76 probe cell. Everything from here shifts +2 (old 24 \u2192 26). |\n| 24\u219226 \u2026 41\u219243 | \u2014 | unchanged (metrics, Lightning module, seed, loggers) |\n| **42\u219244** | code (`exec 24`) | **Edit the Trainer.** Replace `precision=\"bf16-mixed\",` with `precision=cfg.precision,` and add `accumulate_grad_batches=cfg.accumulate_grad_batches,` and `num_sanity_val_steps=0,`. Comment the third: the sanity check runs under `no_grad` so its own peak is small, but it grows the caching allocator's pool with a differently-shaped set of blocks before training's first allocation, and it costs two wasted 832 MiB host-to-device copies out of a 10-sample dataset. Also delete `max_epochs = 2` in favour of `cfg.max_epochs` \u2014 or keep it and say in the comment that it deliberately overrides the config, because right now it silently shadows `max_epochs: 20`. |\n| **41\u219243** | markdown | **Edit.** Currently ends \"Note that in this notebook we also set a mixed precision to reduce the model's footprint in memory.\" Replace with the real story, which is more interesting and is the thing a workshop attendee should leave with: mixed precision on this GPU is *both* a memory and a speed lever, and the correct dtype is hardware-dependent. Give the measured numbers \u2014 T4/SM75 at this model's MLP GEMM shape: fp16 9.3 ms, fp32 56.8 ms, bf16 90.1 ms, because `torch.cuda.is_bf16_supported(including_emulation=False)` is `False` below SM80. The notebook currently teaches \"bf16 = good\", which is wrong on the hardware the workshop runs on. |\n| **44\u219246** | code (`exec 25`) | No source change. Clear the stale error output. |\n| **45\u219247** | markdown | Optionally append a short \"what we changed and what each was worth\" table from the probe numbers. |\n\n### Does `downstream_apps/sf_triggers/3_finetune_template_1D.py` need the mirror change?\n\n**Yes \u2014 all of it.** It is the production path for the same config (`DEFAULT_CONFIG = Path(__file__).parent / \"configs\" / \"config_script.yaml\"`, line 58) and builds the same `HelioSpectformer2D` (line 164-170), so it OOMs identically. Four edits:\n\n1. **line 34 block** \u2014 add `os.environ.setdefault(\"PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF\", \"expandable_segments:True\")` next to the existing `CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG` line.\n2. **lines 51-56** \u2014 add `cast_frozen_to` and `disable_peft_input_dtype_cast` to the `workshop_infrastructure.utils` import.\n3. **`build_model`, after line 186** (`model = apply_peft_lora(...)`) \u2014 add both calls, before `_log_trainable_parameters(model)` at line 188 so the log shows the final state.\n4. **`build_trainer`, line 241** \u2014 `precision=cfg.precision if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"32-true\"`, plus `accumulate_grad_batches=cfg.accumulate_grad_batches` and `num_sanity_val_steps=0` in the same `L.Trainer(...)` call (lines 236-251).\n\n### And `downstream_apps/template/3_finetune_template_1D.py`?\n\n**Partially, and deliberately so.** It builds `HelioSpectformer1D` (line 160-161), which collapses to one CLS token and has no memory problem. But it is the file CLAUDE.md names as the canonical fine-tuning entry point, and forks inherit from it. Apply edits 1, 2 and 4 (the `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF` line, the import, and `precision=cfg.precision` at line 238 \u2014 otherwise the new config key is silently ignored in the template, which is exactly the failure mode `_check_section_keys` exists to prevent). Apply edit 3 too: both helpers are no-ops when they have nothing to do (`disable_peft_input_dtype_cast` on a model without adapters, `cast_frozen_to` under full fine-tuning), and having them present is how the next 2D fork gets them for free.\n\nAlso: the diff between the two scripts is already only ~10 lines (I checked). Keeping edits 1/2/4 identical in both preserves that, which is the property that makes the sf_triggers copy reviewable at all.\n\n---\n\n# Critical Files for Implementation\n\n- `/home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/utils.py` \u2014 add `disable_peft_input_dtype_cast()` and `ca",
