              {
                "path": "/home/jovyan/.claude/plans/adi-wdir-surya-workshop-downstream-apps-cozy-hickey.md",
                "new_text": "# Fix the CUDA OOM in `sf_triggers/2_finetune_template_1D.ipynb`\n\n## Context\n\n`downstream_apps/sf_triggers/2_finetune_template_1D.ipynb` dies at the `trainer.fit(...)` cell\n(JSON index 44, `exec_count 25`) with:\n\n```\nOutOfMemoryError: CUDA out of memory. Tried to allocate 1.25 GiB. GPU 0 has a total capacity\nof 14.74 GiB of which 952.19 MiB is free. ... 11.78 GiB is allocated by PyTorch, and 1.82 GiB\nis reserved by PyTorch but unallocated.\n```\n\nThe obvious knobs are already at floor: `batch_size: 1`, `lora_config.r: 2`, `max_samples: 10`,\n`checkpoint_layers: [0..9]` (all ten). The failure happens *during gradient-checkpoint\nrecomputation in backward*, so there is nothing left to gain from checkpointing either.\n\nThe hardware is a single **Tesla T4, 14.74 GiB usable, compute capability 7.5**. Right now\n`torch.cuda.mem_get_info` reports only **2.55 GiB free** \u2014 12.2 GiB is still held by the dead\nkernel (PID 662721), so no retry can even reproduce the original conditions until the kernel is\nrestarted.\n\n### Root cause (verified, not inferred)\n\n`img_size 4096 / patch_size 16` \u2192 65,536 tokens; `embed_dim 1280`; `mlp_ratio 4` \u2192 MLP hidden\n5120. So `1 \u00d7 65536 \u00d7 5120 \u00d7 4 B` = **exactly 1.25 GiB** \u2014 the failing allocation is\n`mlp.fc2`'s input tensor **in fp32**.\n\nThe Trainer runs `precision=\"bf16-mixed\"`, so that activation *is* bf16. PEFT upcasts it back:\n`peft/tuners/lora/layer.py:805` calls `_cast_input_dtype(x, lora_A.weight.dtype)`, and the\nadapter weights are fp32. The copy is pure waste \u2014 autocast immediately casts it back down for\n`lora_A`. Reproduced directly against the installed PEFT 0.18.1:\n\n```\ncast ENABLED (current): {'in': bfloat16, 'out': float32, 'same_obj': False}\ncast DISABLED (fix):    {'in': bfloat16, 'out': bfloat16, 'same_obj': True}\n```\n\nThis app uses `HelioSpectformer2D`, which keeps the whole `(1, 65536, 1280)` token grid alive\nthrough a 4096\u00d74096 decoder; the template's `HelioSpectformer1D` collapses to one CLS token\nbefore its head (`helio_spectformer.py:293`), which is why the template notebook does not OOM\nand this one does.\n\n### Three things that are counter-intuitive and drive the plan\n\n1. **bf16 is the wrong dtype for this GPU.** `torch.cuda.is_bf16_supported(including_emulation=False)`\n   returns **`False`** on SM75 \u2014 bf16 is emulated. Measured at this model's MLP GEMM shape\n   (16384\u00d71280\u00d75120): **fp16 9.3 ms, fp32 56.8 ms, bf16 90.1 ms**. The current `bf16-mixed`\n   setting pays a 60% slowdown versus plain fp32 for its memory saving. `16-mixed` gives\n   identical memory at ~6\u00d7 the speed, and fp16's 10-bit mantissa rounds the pretrained weights\n   *more* accurately than bf16's 7-bit (0.018% vs 0.141% mean relative error on `complex_weight`).\n\n2. **A fp32 *buffer* pins the entire residual stream to fp32.** `LinearEmbedding.pos_embed`\n   is a `register_buffer` (`embedding.py:113`) of shape `(1, 65536, 1280)` = 320 MiB, added to\n   the half-precision patch-embed output at `embedding.py:124`. `half + fp32 \u2192 fp32`, so all\n   ten blocks carry an fp32 token stream, and with every layer checkpointed that is 11 live\n   `(1,65536,1280)` tensors: **3.44 GiB fp32 vs 1.72 GiB half**. Casting frozen params *and\n   buffers* to fp16 is a ~2.5 GiB win.\n\n3. **`lora_dropout: 0.1` costs 1.4\u20132.2 GiB.** PEFT builds `nn.Dropout` when `p > 0` and\n   `nn.Identity` when `p == 0` (`peft/tuners/lora/layer.py:189-191`). At `p=0.1` every adapted\n   layer allocates a dropout output *plus* a bool mask; on `fc2` that output is the\n   `(1,65536,5120)` tensor again. At `r=2` with 8 training samples the regularization is\n   worthless. One YAML character.\n\n### Explicitly rejected\n\n- **`bf16-true` / `16-true`.** `Strategy.setup` calls `convert_module` *before*\n  `setup_optimizers` (`lightning/pytorch/strategies/strategy.py:154,159`), so Adam's\n  `exp_avg`/`exp_avg_sq` live in bf16, where the `beta2=0.999` update falls below the mantissa\n  resolution and stops accumulating. Measured at lr 1e-4 over 20 steps: a parameter of\n  magnitude 1.0 **does not move at all**. Silent failure with a plausible-looking loss curve \u2014\n  unacceptable in a teaching repo. Mixed precision keeps an fp32 master copy for free.\n- **Reducing resolution / interpolating `complex_weight` off its (256,129) grid.** Not needed:\n  the plan below leaves ~6 GiB of headroom. Do not spend pretrained fidelity unnecessarily.\n- **`prefetch_factor` as an OOM fix.** It is a *host* RAM knob worth 0 bytes of VRAM. It is a\n  real bug (it is what produced the four `terminate called without an active exception` lines\n  in the failed cell's output), fixed below as robustness \u2014 not credited with any GiB.\n- **`pl_simple_baseline.py:194`'s `unsqueeze(1).float()`.** Looks like a shape bug for a 2D\n  target, but `FlareMetrics` does `mse_loss(preds.reshape(-1), target.reshape(-1))`\n  (`sf_triggers_metrics.py:65`), so there is no broadcast, no warning, and no extra copy.\n  Leave it; changing it would break the shared 1D/2D contract for no observable gain.\n\nTwo decisions made without you, since `AskUserQuestion` is unavailable in this session: I\nplanned for **T4-only** (no larger GPU), and I included the shared-infra plumbing per CLAUDE.md's\nreusability directive. Phase 1+2 alone unblock the notebook if you want to stop there.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 0 \u2014 prerequisites\n\n1. **Restart the notebook kernel.** Non-negotiable: 12.2 GiB is still held.\n2. `workshop_infrastructure/utils.py` \u2014 add the two helpers below.\n3. Notebook cell 3, inside the existing pre-import block under\n   `os.environ.setdefault(\"CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG\", \":4096:8\")`:\n   ```python\n   os.environ.setdefault(\"PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF\", \"expandable_segments:True\")\n   ```\n   Recovers the 1.82 GiB of reserved-but-unallocated memory seen in the error. Unlike\n   `CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG` this only has to precede the first CUDA *allocation*, but it\n   belongs in the same \"before torch does anything\" block.\n4. Run cells 2\u219223 plus the new probe cell to get a **baseline number**.\n\n### New helpers in `workshop_infrastructure/utils.py` (after `apply_peft_lora`, ~line 380)\n\n```python\ndef disable_peft_input_dtype_cast(model: torch.nn.Module) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Stop PEFT from upcasting activations to the adapter weight dtype.\n\n    ``peft.tuners.lora.Linear.forward`` calls ``_cast_input_dtype(x, lora_A.weight.dtype)``\n    before the adapter branch.  Under mixed precision the adapter weights are fp32 while ``x``\n    is fp16/bf16, so PEFT allocates a fresh fp32 copy -- and autocast then casts it straight\n    back down for ``lora_A``.  The copy buys nothing, and it is charged at the widest point in\n    the network: for HelioSpectformer2D at 4096x4096, ``mlp.fc2``'s input is (1, 65536, 5120),\n    so the copy is exactly 1.25 GiB, per block, on every gradient-checkpoint recompute.\n\n    ``peft.helpers.disable_input_dtype_casting`` does the same thing as a context manager,\n    which cannot wrap ``trainer.fit()`` cleanly.  This sets the flag once.\n\n    Only meaningful under autocast (``precision`` ending in ``-mixed``); under ``*-true`` the\n    adapter weights are already half, so PEFT skips the copy anyway and this is a no-op.\n\n    Returns the number of tuner layers touched, so an ineffective call is visible in the log.\n    \"\"\"\n    from peft.tuners.tuners_utils import BaseTunerLayer\n\n    touched = 0\n    for module in model.modules():\n        if isinstance(module, BaseTunerLayer):\n            module.cast_input_dtype_enabled = False\n            touched += 1\n    print(f\"[LoRA] Disabled PEFT input-dtype casting on {touched} tuner layer(s).\")\n    return touched\n\n\ndef cast_frozen_to(model: torch.nn.Module, dtype: torch.dtype) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"Cast every frozen parameter and every float buffer to ``dtype``.\n\n    Trainable parameters are left in fp32 so the optimizer keeps a full-precision master copy;\n    under autocast the two dtypes coexist.\n\n    Beyond halving the 1.37 GiB of frozen weights, this fixes two specific things:\n\n      * ``LinearEmbedding.pos_embed`` is a (1, 65536, 1280) *buffer*, not a parameter -- 320 MiB\n        in fp32.  ``embedding.py:124`` adds it to the half-precision patch-embed output, and\n        ``half + fp32 -> fp32``, which pins the inter-block residual stream to fp32 for all ten\n        blocks.  With every layer checkpointed that is 11 live token tensors: 3.44 GiB fp32\n        versus 1.72 GiB half.\n      * ``complex_weight`` (169 M elements, 645 MiB fp32) halves.  It is upcast back to fp32\n        inside ``SpectralGatingNetwork.forward`` for the FFT, so the rounding is permanent:\n        0.018% mean relative error in fp16 (0.141% in bf16).  Below the noise floor of mixed\n        precision, but it *is* a change to a pretrained tensor -- hence the print.\n\n    Call after ``load_pretrained_weights()`` and ``apply_peft_lora()``, so the checkpoint is read\n    at full precision and rounded exactly once and ``requires_grad`` already reflects the chosen\n    regime.  Under full fine-tuning nothing is frozen and this is a no-op.\n    \"\"\"\n    n_params = 0\n    for param in model.parameters():\n        if not param.requires_grad and param.is_floating_point() and param.dtype != dtype:\n            param.data = param.data.to(dtype)\n            n_params += 1\n\n    n_buffers = 0\n    for buf in model.buffers():\n        if buf.is_floating_point() and buf.dtype != dtype:\n            buf.data = buf.data.to(dtype)\n            n_buffers += 1\n\n    print(f\"[PRECISION] Cast {n_params} frozen parameter(s) and {n_buffers} buffer(s) to {dtype}.\")\n    return n_params, n_buffers\n```\n\nKeep these as two separate functions rather than folding them into `apply_peft_lora` \u2014\n`apply_peft_lora` must not silently depend on a precision chosen later when the Trainer is built.\n\n**Safety audit** (all verified against the installed torch, with half weights under autocast):\n`Conv2d` patch embed \u2713, `x + pos_embed` half+half \u2713, `LayerNorm` (autocast promotes to fp32) \u2713,\n`complex_weight.to(torch.float32)` \u2192 `view_as_complex` \u2713, `torch.fft.rfft2` never sees half\n(`spectformer.py:61` upcasts first \u2014 a direct half input *does* raise), all `Linear`s \u2713,\n`Conv2d`+`PixelShuffle` head (trainable, stays fp32) \u2713, `DropPath` is `Identity` \u2713.\n\n**One trap:** after `cast_frozen_to`, a bare `model.forward(...)` with no autocast raises\n`expected scalar type Half but found Float` from the patch-embed Conv2d. Cell 23's smoke test\nmust be wrapped \u2014 see the notebook edits.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1 \u2014 strictly necessary (~5.5 GiB)\n\n| step | change | expected \u0394peak |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1.1 | `disable_peft_input_dtype_cast(model)` after `apply_peft_lora` | \u22122.5 to \u22123.1 GiB |\n| 1.2 | `lora_dropout: 0.0` in `sf_triggers/configs/config_script.yaml` | \u22121.4 to \u22122.2 GiB |\n| 1.3 | `precision: 16-mixed` (config + Trainer cell) | 0 GiB, **~6\u00d7 faster GEMMs** |\n\nRe-probe. Expect ~11\u201312 GiB peak \u2014 this alone should fit, but do Phase 2 anyway: it is free.\n\n## Phase 2 \u2014 recommended (~2.5 GiB)\n\n| step | change | expected \u0394peak |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2.1 | `cast_frozen_to(model, torch.float16)` after 1.1 | \u22122.5 GiB (params 0.67 + `pos_embed` 0.16 + residual stream 1.72) |\n\nRe-probe. Expect **~8.5\u20139 GiB, ~6 GiB headroom**. Stop here and train.\n\n## Phase 3 \u2014 only if Phase 2 still does not fit\n\n| step | change | \u0394peak |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3.1 | `.to(k.dtype)` on the two `dual_ln_full` calls, `transformer_ls.py:105-106` | \u22121.3 GiB. Vendored edit \u2014 record it; per CLAUDE.md there is no diff signal against upstream |\n| 3.2 | `ft_checkpoint_head=True` (new `HelioSpectformer2D` ctor arg, off by default) | \u2212160 MiB |\n| 3.3 | `accumulate_grad_batches: 2` | 0 GiB of peak, but raises the effective batch \u2014 the real scientific problem with 8 samples |\n\n### Stopping rule \u2014 if Phase 2 still OOMs\n\nOne of the dtype assumptions is wrong for this build, and the probe says which. Check in order:\n1. `peak reserved \u2212 peak allocated` > 2 GiB \u2192 `expandable_segments` did not take effect\n   (confirm with `torch.cuda.memory.get_allocator_backend()`).\n2. Static footprint > 1.0 GiB \u2192 `cast_frozen_to` did not fire; check its printed count.\n3. A 1.25 GiB step still visible \u2192 the PEFT cast is still on; if the helper printed 0 layers,\n   PEFT changed its class hierarchy.\n4. All clean and still OOM \u2192 print `tokens.dtype` inside `SpectFormer.forward`\n   (`spectformer.py:288`); if fp32, something else is promoting the residual stream.\n\nOnly after all four come back clean is resolution reduction on the table.\n\n---\n\n## Files to modify\n\n### `workshop_infrastructure/utils.py`\nAdd `disable_peft_input_dtype_cast()` and `cast_frozen_to()` as above. `apply_peft_lora`,\n`discover_head_modules`, and the `head_` convention are untouched, so `tests/test_lora_setup.py`\nstays valid.\n\n### `workshop_infrastructure/configs.py` \u2014 four edits, all required\nCLAUDE.md's \"unknown keys raise\" property means a YAML key without a dataclass field is a hard\nerror, so all four must land together.\n1. New `VALID_PRECISION = (\"32-true\", \"16-mixed\", \"bf16-mixed\", \"bf16-true\")` after\n   `VALID_DETERMINISTIC` (line 52), with a comment carrying the measured T4 GEMM numbers and the\n   `bf16-true`/Adam warning.\n2. `TrainingConfig` fields after `dtype` (line 254): `precision: str = \"16-mixed\"` and\n   `accumulate_grad_batches: int = 1`. Note in the comment that `precision` is the *only*\n   precision control that does anything \u2014 `dtype` is inert (`helio_spectformer.py:80`).\n3. The `TrainingConfig(...)` construction (~line 428): `precision=training.get(\"precision\", \"16-mixed\")`,\n   `accumulate_grad_batches=training.get(\"accumulate_grad_batches\", 1)`.\n4. `_TRAINING_KEYS` (lines 436-439): add both names.\nPlus `__post_init__` validation in the existing style of the `deterministic` check \u2014 reject a\n`precision` outside `VALID_PRECISION` naming the alternatives, and a non-integer or `< 1`\n`accumulate_grad_batches`.\n\n### `workshop_infrastructure/models/finetune_models.py` (Phase 3 only)\nAdd `ft_checkpoint_head: bool = False` ctor arg \u2192 `self.checkpoint_head`, and in `forward`\n(lines 304-306) wrap `self.head_unembed` in\n`checkpoint(self.head_unembed, tokens, use_reentrant=False)` when\n`self.checkpoint_head and self.training and torch.is_grad_enabled()`. Gate it on the ctor arg\npassed through `from_config(**overrides)` \u2014 the channel `ft_out_chans`/`ft_unembedding_type`\nalready use \u2014 **not** a new `ModelConfig` field, so `ModelConfig.__post_init__`'s\n`checkpoint_layers`-vs-`depth` invariant and `tests/test_model_config.py` stay untouched.\n`use_reentrant=False` matches the backbone (`spectformer.py:292`, `helio_spectformer.py:288`);\ndo not mix flavours. This touches neither of the two independent backbone checkpoint loops \u2014\n`HelioSpectformer2D` routes through `SpectFormer.forward`, and `forward_with_cls_token` is the\n1D-only path with no decoder to checkpoint.\n\n### `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py` \u2014 host RAM, 0 VRAM\nAdd a `prefetch_factor: int | None = None` parameter and set it inside the existing\n`if workers > 0:` block (DataLoader rejects it at `num_workers == 0`). Document the arithmetic in\nthe docstring: one sample is `(13, T, 4096, 4096)` fp32 = 832 MiB, so 4 workers \u00d7 prefetch 2 \u00d7\nbatch 1 is 6.5 GiB pinned host RAM per loader \u2014 and because the same `loader_kwargs` dict is\nreused for validation with `persistent_workers=True`, both loaders hold that simultaneously.\nState plainly that it will not move `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()`.\n**Do not** add it to `TrainingConfig`: it is a per-machine knob like `num_workers` and\n`s3_cache_dir`, and `build_helio_dataloaders` already takes call-site overrides for that class.\n\n### `downstream_apps/sf_triggers/configs/config_script.yaml`\n`lora_config.lora_dropout: 0.1` \u2192 `0.0`; add `precision: 16-mixed` and\n`accumulate_grad_batches: 1` to `training:`, with comments giving the measured fp16/fp32/bf16\ntimings and why bf16 is wrong below SM80.\n\n### `downstream_apps/sf_triggers/3_finetune_template_1D.py` \u2014 needs all of it\nSame config, same `HelioSpectformer2D` (lines 164-170), so it OOMs identically.\n1. Add the `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF` line next to `CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG` (line 34 block).\n2. Import both new helpers (lines 51-56).\n3. In `build_model`, after `apply_peft_lora` (line 186) and *before*\n   `_log_trainable_parameters(model)` (line 188), call both.\n4. In `build_trainer` (lines 236-251): `precision=cfg.precision if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"32-true\"`,\n   plus `accumulate_grad_batches=cfg.accumulate_grad_batches` and `num_sanity_val_steps=0`.\n\n### `downstream_apps/template/3_finetune_template_1D.py` \u2014 edits 1, 2, 4 and 3\nIt builds `HelioSpectformer1D` and has no memory problem, but it is the canonical entry point\nCLAUDE.md names and forks inherit from it. `precision=cfg.precision` in particular must land here\nor the new config key is silently ignored in the template \u2014 exactly the failure mode\n`_check_section_keys` exists to prevent. Both helpers are no-ops when they have nothing to do, so\nedit 3 is safe and gives the next 2D fork the fix for free. The two scripts currently differ by\n~10 lines; keeping these edits identical preserves that reviewability.\n\n### `tests/test_model_config.py`\nA valid `precision` round-trips; an invalid one raises naming the alternatives; an unknown\n`training:` key still raises. That last one guards against someone adding to `_TRAINING_KEYS`\nwithout the dataclass field, and vice versa.\n\n### Docs\nAdd a short memory-troubleshooting section (`CLAUDE.md` and/or `ADAPTING.md`): the repo currently\nhas no VRAM guidance at all. Cover the 1D-vs-2D token-grid difference, the four levers with their\nmeasured sizes, `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF`, and the hardware-dependent precision choice.\n\n---\n\n## Notebook edits \u2014 `downstream_apps/sf_triggers/2_finetune_template_1D.ipynb`\n\n46 cells, `trainer.fit` at index 44. The notebook is `M` in `git status`, so **re-read it before\nediting** rather than trusting these indices. Insertions shift later cells \u2014 apply bottom-up.\n\n| idx | action |\n|---|---|\n| **3** | Add the `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF` line; import the two new helpers. Do not reorder anything else in this cell. |\n| **11** | Add `prefetch_factor=1,` next to `num_workers=4,`, with a comment noting it is host RAM (and is what the four `terminate called without an active exception` lines were), *not* GPU. |\n| **20** | Fix a drifted claim: this markdown says `apply_peft_lora()` \"re-enables gradients for every parameter outside `backbone.*`\". It does not \u2014 it passes `discover_head_modules(model)` to PEFT as `modules_to_save` (`utils.py:323,337`); the `head_` prefix *is* the mechanism. The template's copy is correct. Then add a paragraph on the two precision levers and why the fp32 upcast exists \u2014 the teachable moment of the whole exercise. |\n| **21** | After `apply_peft_lora`: `disable_peft_input_dtype_cast(model)` (inside the `use_lora` branch) and `cast_frozen_to(model, torch.float16)` (outside it, so it also helps the linear-probe regime). Comment that the latter must follow `load_pretrained_weights` (cell 19), and that `torch.bfloat16` is the right argument on SM80+. |\n| **22** | One sentence: the frozen half is now fp16, so any bare `model.forward(...)` needs `torch.autocast`. |\n| **23** | **Required, not optional** \u2014 this cell breaks after 2.1. Wrap the forward in `torch.autocast(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\", dtype=torch.float16)`. Note in the comment that it still runs on CPU, and that `output` must keep its autograd graph because cells 28/30/32 consume it \u2014 do not add `no_grad`. |\n| **24** | **INSERT** markdown: why a static parameter count does not predict an OOM, and why the peak lives inside a checkpoint recompute. |\n| **25** | **INSERT** the memory-probe cell (below). |\n| **43** (was 41) | Rewrite the markdown. It currently teaches \"bf16 = good\", which is wrong on the hardware this workshop runs on. Give the measured numbers: T4/SM75 at this model's MLP GEMM shape \u2014 fp16 9.3 ms, fp32 56.8 ms, bf16 90.1 ms, because `is_bf16_supported(including_emulation=False)` is `False` below SM80. Mixed precision is a memory *and* speed lever, and the right dtype is hardware-dependent. |\n| **44** (was 42) | Trainer: `precision=\"bf16-mixed\"` \u2192 `precision=cfg.precision`; add `accumulate_grad_batches=cfg.accumulate_grad_batches` and `num_sanity_val_steps=0` (the sanity check runs under `no_grad`, but it grows the allocator pool with differently-shaped blocks before training's first allocation and wastes two 832 MiB H2D copies out of a 10-sample dataset). Also: `max_epochs = 2` silently shadows `max_epochs: 20` from the config \u2014 either use `cfg.max_epochs` or say in the comment that the override is deliberate. |\n| **46** (was 44) | No source change; clear the stale error output. |\n\n### Memory-probe cell (new, index 25)\n\nInsert *after* the existing forward-pass smoke test (index 23) \u2014 `output`/`batch` are live there\nand cells 28/30/32 consume `output`, so the probe must not disturb either. Everything is scoped\nto a temporary GPU copy and torn down, leaving the pool clean for `trainer.fit`.\n\n```python\n# Measure, don't guess: one real forward+backward, peak GPU memory reported.\n#\n# Lightning prints \"1,434.807 MB total estimated model params size\" and stops there -- the\n# *static* cost. What OOMs a 15 GB T4 is the transient cost of one gradient-checkpoint\n# recompute, which nothing in the training log shows you. Run this once per lever you change\n# and record the number.\nimport gc\nimport torch\n\nPROBE_PRECISION = torch.float16   # match training.precision: 16-mixed on a T4 (SM75 has native\n                                  # fp16 tensor cores; bf16 is EMULATED and slower than fp32).\n                                  # Use torch.bfloat16 on SM80+.\n\ndef probe_step_memory(model, batch, autocast_dtype=PROBE_PRECISION, label=\"\"):\n    \"\"\"Run one forward+backward on GPU and report peak allocated/reserved memory.\"\"\"\n    dev = torch.device(\"cuda\")\n    model = model.to(dev)\n    gpu_batch = {k: (v.to(dev, non_blocking=True) if torch.is_tensor(v) else v)\n                 for k, v in batch.items()}\n\n    model.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\n    gc.collect()\n    torch.cuda.empty_cache()\n    torch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()\n    base = torch.cuda.memory_allocated()\n\n    # autocast is not optional: with a half-precision frozen backbone and fp32 adapters,\n    # autocast is what reconciles the two dtypes.\n    with torch.autocast(\"cuda\", dtype=autocast_dtype):\n        out = model(gpu_batch)\n        loss = torch.nn.functional.mse_loss(\n            out.reshape(-1), gpu_batch[\"forecast\"].reshape(-1).float()\n        )\n    loss.backward()\n\n    peak_alloc = torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() / 2**30\n    peak_resv = torch.cuda.max_memory_reserved() / 2**30\n    total = torch.cuda.get_device_properties(0).total_memory / 2**30\n    print(f\"{label or 'probe'}:\")\n    print(f\"  static (model on GPU, pre-step) : {base / 2**30:6.2f} GiB\")\n    print(f\"  peak allocated                  : {peak_alloc:6.2f} GiB\")\n    print(f\"  peak reserved                   : {peak_resv:6.2f} GiB\")\n    print(f\"  reserved-but-unallocated (frag) : {peak_resv - peak_alloc:6.2f} GiB\")\n    print(f\"  device total                    : {total:6.2f} GiB \"\n          f\"-> headroom {total - peak_resv:5.2f} GiB\")\n    print(f\"  loss                            : {loss.item():.6f}  ({out.dtype})\")\n\n    del out, loss, gpu_batch\n    model.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\n    model.to(\"cpu\")          # leaving 1.4 GB resident reliably OOMs the first real step\n    gc.collect()\n    torch.cuda.empty_cache()\n    return peak_alloc\n\n_ = probe_step_memory(model, batch, label=\"baseline\")\n```\n\n---\n\n## Verification\n\n1. **Unit tests** (CPU, fast): `pytest tests/ -v`. `tests/test_lora_setup.py` pins the trainable\n   counts (LoRA 3,157,761 / probe 1,642,241) and must stay green \u2014 the helpers change dtypes and\n   a PEFT flag, not `requires_grad` or module structure. `tests/test_model_config.py` gains the\n   `precision` cases.\n2. **Config load**: `load_flare_config(\"./configs/config_script.yaml\")` accepts the two new keys;\n   an invalid `precision` raises naming the alternatives; an unknown `training:` key still raises.\n3. **Probe ladder in the notebook** \u2014 the real verification. Record `peak allocated` at baseline,\n   after Phase 1, and after Phase 2. Expected: OOM/~13 GiB \u2192 ~11\u201312 GiB \u2192 ~8.5\u20139 GiB. If a phase\n   does not move the number roughly as predicted, that helper did not fire; its printed counts say\n   so.\n4. **Full notebook run**: restart kernel, run all cells. `trainer.fit` completes 2 epochs on the\n   10-sample dataset. Confirm the loss actually decreases and that `head_unembed`'s weights move\n   (they are fp32, so they will) \u2014 with `16-mixed` the `GradScaler` can skip steps on non-finite\n   gradients, and a flat loss with a collapsing grad scale is the signature to watch for on a\n   10-sample run.\n5. **Script path**: `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python -m downstream_apps.sf_triggers.3_finetune_template_1D --max-epochs 2 --no-wandb`\n   with `max_samples` capped, confirming the mirrored fix. Then the same for\n   `downstream_apps.template.3_finetune_template_1D` to confirm the 1D path is unbroken by the\n   config change.\n6. **`nvidia-smi`** after each run: no leaked memory, no orphaned worker processes.\n"
              }
            ],
