      "time": 1789675952.4806924,
      "mentions": [],
      "body": "`dual_ln_full` returns **fp32** under autocast (LayerNorm always does), so k and v become fp32 before the 4\u00d7 overlapping-tile materialization \u2014 that's the dominant term. Note the plan's suggested `.to(k.dtype)` would be a no-op (k *is* the fp32 LayerNorm output); the compute dtype lives on `q`. Let me make the edit and measure:",
      "id": "f6a5b39b-33ab-4139-b5eb-e5f214abe5dc"
    },
    {
      "id": "38278ee2-d17f-48fd-b7fc-fdd05b786962",
      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "metadata": {
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "kind": "execute",
            "title": "cd surya_workshop && python - <<'PYEOF' from pathlib import Path p = Path(\"workshop_infrastructure/models/transformer_ls.py\") s = p.read_text() old = ''' # Layer norm on the projected keys and values k = self.dual_ln_full(k) v = self.dual_ln_full(v)''' new = ''' # Layer norm on the projected keys and values. # # The .to(q.dtype) is a memory fix, not a numerics change. Under autocast LayerNorm # always returns fp32, while q here is the autocast compute dtype (it came out of # self.qkv, an autocast-eligible Linear). Leaving k and v in fp32 makes every # downstream tensor fp32 -- including get_overlapping_tiles(), which .contiguous() # materializes a ~4x expansion of both. At 4096x4096 that is 1.25 GiB per tile set # in fp32 versus 0.62 GiB in half. The matmuls that consume them would be run in # half by autocast either way, so casting here only moves the cast earlier, ahead of # the expansion instead of after it. The norm itself is still computed in fp32. # # Vendored file: this is a local change with no upstream diff signal. See the GPU # Memory section of CLAUDE.md. k = self.dual_ln_full(k).to(q.dtype) v = self.dual_ln_full(v).to(q.dtype)''' assert s.count(old) == 1 p.write_text(s.replace(old, new)) print(\"ok\") PYEOF PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True python real_probe.py 2>&1 | grep -viE \"futurewarning|warnings.warn|Warning: PYTORCH\" | tail -6",
            "raw_input": {
              "command": "cd /home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop && /home/jovyan/envs/surya_ws/bin/python - <<'PYEOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"workshop_infrastructure/models/transformer_ls.py\")\ns = p.read_text()\nold = '''        # Layer norm on the projected keys and values\n        k = self.dual_ln_full(k)\n        v = self.dual_ln_full(v)'''\nnew = '''        # Layer norm on the projected keys and values.\n        #\n        # The .to(q.dtype) is a memory fix, not a numerics change. Under autocast LayerNorm\n        # always returns fp32, while q here is the autocast compute dtype (it came out of\n        # self.qkv, an autocast-eligible Linear). Leaving k and v in fp32 makes every\n        # downstream tensor fp32 -- including get_overlapping_tiles(), which .contiguous()\n        # materializes a ~4x expansion of both. At 4096x4096 that is 1.25 GiB per tile set\n        # in fp32 versus 0.62 GiB in half. The matmuls that consume them would be run in\n        # half by autocast either way, so casting here only moves the cast earlier, ahead of\n        # the expansion instead of after it. The norm itself is still computed in fp32.\n        #\n        # Vendored file: this is a local change with no upstream diff signal. See the GPU\n        # Memory section of CLAUDE.md.\n        k = self.dual_ln_full(k).to(q.dtype)\n        v = self.dual_ln_full(v).to(q.dtype)'''\nassert s.count(old) == 1\np.write_text(s.replace(old, new))\nprint(\"ok\")\nPYEOF\nPYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True /home/jovyan/envs/surya_ws/bin/python /tmp/real_probe.py 2>&1 | grep -viE \"futurewarning|warnings.warn|Warning: PYTORCH\" | tail -6",
              "description": "Apply dual_ln dtype fix and measure"
            },
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_0181mrPyRSmwWbA5U2H8QXrc",
            "status": "completed",
            "raw_output": "ok\nconfig: img_size=4096 patch=16 embed=1280 depth=10 checkpoint_layers=10 batch=1\n\n(a) BEFORE: bf16-mixed, PEFT cast ON, lora_dropout 0.1\n    peak before failure 13.37 GiB  -> OOM: CUDA out of memory\n(b) AFTER:  16-mixed, cast OFF, dropout 0.0, cast_frozen_to(fp16)\n    static  0.84 | peak 10.05 | reserved 11.93 | frag 1.87 | headroom  2.82 GiB  -> RAN\nShell cwd was reset to /home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/models"
          }
