        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "raw_input": {
              "description": "Replace predicted numbers with measured ones",
              "command": "cd /home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop && cat > /tmp/measured.py <<'PYEOF'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# 1. finetune_models.py -- the estimate was wrong; measured it buys nothing here.\np = Path(\"workshop_infrastructure/models/finetune_models.py\")\ns = p.read_text()\nold = '''            # The decoder's saved activations are modest next to a backbone block: one\n            # (B, L, D)-sized contiguous copy that einops' reshape+permute forces Conv2d\n            # to make, plus two (B, 1, H, W) tensors -- roughly 320 MiB in fp32, 160 MiB in\n            # half at 4096x4096.  Off by default because the recompute is a poor trade\n            # unless you are within that margin of fitting.'''\nnew = '''            # Off by default, and measured worth 0 GiB at the sf_triggers config (4096x4096,\n            # batch 1, all 10 backbone layers checkpointed): peak was 10.05 GiB either way.\n            # The decoder's saved activations are real -- one (B, L, D) contiguous copy that\n            # einops' reshape+permute forces Conv2d to make, plus two (B, 1, H, W) tensors --\n            # but they do not coexist with the peak, which sits inside a backbone attention\n            # block's recompute. Kept for configs where the head is larger relative to the\n            # backbone (a PerceiverDecoder, more out_chans, or fewer checkpointed layers).\n            # Measure before enabling it; the recompute is not free.'''\nassert s.count(old) == 1\np.write_text(s.replace(old, new))\nprint(\"ok finetune_models.py\")\n\n# 2. notebook: replace reduced-resolution figure with the real one\nnbp = Path(\"downstream_apps/sf_triggers/2_finetune_template_1D.ipynb\")\nnb = json.loads(nbp.read_text())\nc = nb[\"cells\"][20]\nsrc = \"\".join(c[\"source\"])\nold = \"The same cell applies two precision adjustments, and they are worth understanding rather than copying. Measured on this T4 at reduced resolution they cut peak memory by 29%; at the full 4096\u00d74096 the saving is larger, because the biggest term scales with the token count.\"\nnew = \"The same cell applies two precision adjustments, and they are worth understanding rather than copying. Measured on this T4 at the full 4096\u00d74096 config, the old settings hit `OutOfMemoryError` with 13.37 GiB allocated; with these two changes (plus `16-mixed` and one fix in the attention block) the same step peaks at **10.05 GiB and completes**, with 2.8 GiB of headroom.\"\nassert src.count(old) == 1\nsrc = src.replace(old, new)\nc[\"source\"] = [l + \"\\n\" for l in src.split(\"\\n\")[:-1]] + ([src.split(\"\\n\")[-1]] if src.split(\"\\n\")[-1] else [])\nnbp.write_text(json.dumps(nb, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False) + \"\\n\")\nprint(\"ok notebook\")\n\n# 3. CLAUDE.md -- measured lever table + the vendored transformer_ls change\ncp = Path(\"CLAUDE.md\")\ns = cp.read_text()\nanchor = '''**Host RAM is a separate problem with a similar symptom.**'''\ninsert = '''**Measured, sf_triggers config (4096x4096, batch 1, all 10 layers checkpointed, Tesla T4\n14.74 GiB).** One forward+backward+step, random weights (shapes and dtypes are what matter):\n\n| configuration | peak allocated | outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| `bf16-mixed`, PEFT cast on, `lora_dropout 0.1` | 13.37 GiB | **OOM** |\n| `16-mixed`, cast off, `lora_dropout 0.0`, `cast_frozen_to(fp16)` | 11.44 GiB | runs, 2.35 GiB headroom |\n| + `dual_ln_full(...).to(q.dtype)` | **10.05 GiB** | runs, 2.82 GiB headroom |\n\nIndividual levers, measured rather than estimated:\n- `disable_peft_input_dtype_cast` + `cast_frozen_to` + `16-mixed`: \u22121.9 GiB combined.\n- `dual_ln_full(...).to(q.dtype)` in `transformer_ls.py:105-106` (**a vendored change, no\n  upstream diff signal**): **\u22121.39 GiB**, the single largest lever. `nn.LayerNorm` always\n  returns fp32 under autocast, so leaving `k`/`v` in fp32 makes `get_overlapping_tiles()`\n  `.contiguous()` materialize a ~4\u00d7 expansion of both in fp32 \u2014 1.25 GiB per tile set instead\n  of 0.62 GiB. The consuming matmuls run in half either way, so the cast only moves earlier.\n- `expandable_segments:True`: fragmentation 2.20 GiB \u2192 0.95 GiB, headroom +1.25 GiB.\n- `lora_dropout: 0.1 \u2192 0.0`: ~5 MiB. Kept because it is free, **not** a real lever \u2014 under\n  checkpointing those tensors are transient and never coexist with the peak.\n- `ft_checkpoint_head=True`: **0 GiB** at this config, for the same reason. Off by default.\n\nThe lesson worth carrying: levers that look large in a per-tensor budget are worth nothing if\nthe tensor does not coexist with the peak. Measure with `probe_step_memory()`.\n\n**Host RAM is a separate problem with a similar symptom.**'''\nassert s.count(anchor) == 1\ncp.write_text(s.replace(anchor, insert))\nprint(\"ok CLAUDE.md\")\nPYEOF\n/home/jovyan/envs/surya_ws/bin/python /tmp/measured.py && /home/jovyan/envs/surya_ws/bin/python -m pytest tests/ -q 2>&1 | tail -3"
            },
            "raw_output": "ok finetune_models.py\nok notebook\nok CLAUDE.md\n\n-- Docs: https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/capture-warnings.html\n65 passed, 3 warnings in 4.33s\nShell cwd was reset to /home/jovyan/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/models",
            "title": "cd surya_workshop && cat > measured.py <<'PYEOF' import json from pathlib import Path # 1. finetune_models.py -- the estimate was wrong; measured it buys nothing here. p = Path(\"workshop_infrastructure/models/finetune_models.py\") s = p.read_text() old = ''' # The decoder's saved activations are modest next to a backbone block: one # (B, L, D)-sized contiguous copy that einops' reshape+permute forces Conv2d # to make, plus two (B, 1, H, W) tensors -- roughly 320 MiB in fp32, 160 MiB in # half at 4096x4096. Off by default because the recompute is a poor trade # unless you are within that margin of fitting.''' new = ''' # Off by default, and measured worth 0 GiB at the sf_triggers config (4096x4096, # batch 1, all 10 backbone layers checkpointed): peak was 10.05 GiB either way. # The decoder's saved activations are real -- one (B, L, D) contiguous copy that # einops' reshape+permute forces Conv2d to make, plus two (B, 1, H, W) tensors -- # but they do not coexist with the peak, which sits inside a backbone attention # block's recompute. Kept for configs where the head is larger relative to the # backbone (a PerceiverDecoder, more out_chans, or fewer checkpointed layers). # Measure before enabling it; the recompute is not free.''' assert s.count(old) == 1 p.write_text(s.replace(old, new)) print(\"ok finetune_models.py\") # 2. notebook: replace reduced-resolution figure with the real one nbp = Path(\"downstream_apps/sf_triggers/2_finetune_template_1D.ipynb\") nb = json.loads(nbp.read_text()) c = nb[\"cells\"][20] src = \"\".join(c[\"source\"]) old = \"The same cell applies two precision adjustments, and they are worth understanding rather than copying. Measured on this T4 at reduced resolution they cut peak memory by 29%; at the full 4096\u00d74096 the saving is larger, because the biggest term scales with the token count.\" new = \"The same cell applies two precision adjustments, and they are worth understanding rather than copying. Measured on this T4 at the full 4096\u00d74096 config, the old settings hit `OutOfMemoryError` with 13.37 GiB allocated; with these two changes (plus `16-mixed` and one fix in the attention block) the same step peaks at **10.05 GiB and completes**, with 2.8 GiB of headroom.\" assert src.count(old) == 1 src = src.replace(old, new) c[\"source\"] = [l + \"\\n\" for l in src.split(\"\\n\")[:-1]] + ([src.split(\"\\n\")[-1]] if src.split(\"\\n\")[-1] else []) nbp.write_text(json.dumps(nb, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False) + \"\\n\") print(\"ok notebook\") # 3. CLAUDE.md -- measured lever table + the vendored transformer_ls change cp = Path(\"CLAUDE.md\") s = cp.read_text() anchor = '''**Host RAM is a separate problem with a similar symptom.**''' insert = '''**Measured, sf_triggers config (4096x4096, batch 1, all 10 layers checkpointed, Tesla T4 14.74 GiB).** One forward+backward+step, random weights (shapes and dtypes are what matter): | configuration | peak allocated | outcome | |---|---|---| | `bf16-mixed`, PEFT cast on, `lora_dropout 0.1` | 13.37 GiB | **OOM** | | `16-mixed`, cast off, `lora_dropout 0.0`, `cast_frozen_to(fp16)` | 11.44 GiB | runs, 2.35 GiB headroom | | + `dual_ln_full(...).to(q.dtype)` | **10.05 GiB** | runs, 2.82 GiB headroom | Individual levers, measured rather than estimated: - `disable_peft_input_dtype_cast` + `cast_frozen_to` + `16-mixed`: \u22121.9 GiB combined. - `dual_ln_full(...).to(q.dtype)` in `transformer_ls.py:105-106` (**a vendored change, no upstream diff signal**): **\u22121.39 GiB**, the single largest lever. `nn.LayerNorm` always returns fp32 under autocast, so leaving `k`/`v` in fp32 makes `get_overlapping_tiles()` `.contiguous()` materialize a ~4\u00d7 expansion of both in fp32 \u2014 1.25 GiB per tile set instead of 0.62 GiB. The consuming matmuls run in half either way, so the cast only moves earlier. - `expandable_segments:True`: fragmentation 2.20 GiB \u2192 0.95 GiB, headroom +1.25 GiB. - `lora_dropout: 0.1 \u2192 0.0`: ~5 MiB. Kept because it is free, **not** a real lever \u2014 under checkpointing those tensors are transient and never coexist with the peak. - `ft_checkpoint_head=True`: **0 GiB** at this config, for the same reason. Off by default. The lesson worth carrying: levers that look large in a per-tensor budget are worth nothing if the tensor does not coexist with the peak. Measure with `probe_step_memory()`. **Host RAM is a separate problem with a similar symptom.**''' assert s.count(anchor) == 1 cp.write_text(s.replace(anchor, insert)) print(\"ok CLAUDE.md\") PYEOF python measured.py && python -m pytest tests/ -q 2>&1 | tail -3",
