    {
      "body": "Done and verified end to end.\n\n## The fix works \u2014 real 2-epoch run completed on your T4\n\n```\n`Trainer.fit` stopped: `max_epochs=2` reached.\n[CKPT] Best checkpoint: checkpoints/best-epoch=01-val_loss=0.0739.ckpt\n[CKPT] Best val_loss: 0.073896\n```\n\nExit code 0, no OOM, no worker `terminate called` messages, `val_loss` improving 0.095 \u2192 0.0739 across the two epochs, and the GPU released cleanly to 0 MiB afterwards. All three helpers fired as intended: 36 tuner layers de-casted, 86 frozen params + **1 buffer** (`pos_embed`) to fp16, 36 norm layers held at fp32, trainable params 706,816 \u2014 byte-identical regime to before.\n\n| configuration | peak allocated | outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| your settings (`bf16-mixed`, PEFT cast on) | 13.37 GiB | **OOM** \u2014 reproduced |\n| after the fix | **10.05 GiB** | runs, 2.8 GiB headroom |\n\n**Root cause:** the 1.25 GiB allocation was `mlp.fc2`'s input `(1, 65536, 5120)` in fp32 \u2014 PEFT upcast the bf16 activation to match its fp32 adapter weights, and autocast cast it straight back down. A pure-waste copy, billed at the widest tensor in the network, on every gradient-checkpoint recompute.\n\n## Where measurement overruled the plan\n\n- **bf16 was actively hurting you.** SM75 emulates bf16 \u2014 at this model's MLP GEMM shape: fp16 9.3 ms, fp32 56.8 ms, **bf16 90.1 ms**. Your `bf16-mixed` paid a 60% slowdown versus no mixed precision at all. Now `16-mixed`.\n- **The largest lever wasn't in the plan at all.** `dual_ln_full` returns fp32 (LayerNorm always does under autocast), so `k`/`v` stayed fp32 through a 4\u00d7 tile `.contiguous()` expansion. One `.to(q.dtype)` in `transformer_ls.py:105-106` = **\u22121.39 GiB**, more than everything else combined. The plan's suggested `.to(k.dtype)` would have been a no-op.\n- **Two predicted levers are worthless here.** `lora_dropout: 0.0` saved 5 MiB (predicted 1.4\u20132.2 GiB); `ft_checkpoint_head` saved exactly 0. Under checkpointing those tensors never coexist with the peak. I replaced the overstated numbers in the config comments and docs with measured ones rather than leave them.\n\n`bf16-true` is deliberately excluded \u2014 Lightning casts the module before building the optimizer, so Adam's `exp_avg_sq` lands in bf16 and stops accumulating; a parameter of magnitude 1.0 measurably never moves while the loss curve still looks healthy.\n\n## Two pre-existing bugs found on the way\n\n`downstream_apps/sf_triggers/3_finetune_template_1D.py` **had never been runnable** \u2014 it imported the template's config loader (which rejects this app's four `data:` keys) and passed a `label_transform` its dataset doesn't accept. Both fixed; otherwise my mirrored edits there would have been dead code.\n\nAlso worth knowing: torch 2.9 warns that `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF` is deprecated in favour of `PYTORCH_ALLOC_CONF`. **Don't act on it** \u2014 I verified via `memory_snapshot()[\"is_expandable\"]` that the new name is silently ignored for this setting on 2.9.1. That flag is worth 1.25 GiB of headroom, so it's documented in-line at all three call sites.\n\n## Changed\n\n13 files: two helpers in `utils.py`, a validated `training.precision` / `accumulate_grad_batches` config field, `prefetch_factor` in `builders.py`, the vendored `transformer_ls.py` fix, both entry-point scripts, the notebook (now with a `probe_step_memory()` cell so levers get measured rather than guessed), and a GPU Memory section in CLAUDE.md. **65 tests pass**, up from 56.\n\nOne deviation from the approved plan: `cast_frozen_to` skips normalization layers. The plan's audit said half LayerNorm weights were fine, but that was CUDA-only \u2014 on CPU `F.layer_norm` raises `mixed dtype (CPU)`, which would have broken the notebook's pre-GPU smoke test. Norm params are `2 \u00d7 embed_dim` each, so it costs nothing and is the standard rule anyway.\n\nTwo notes: my verification run left an 892 MB checkpoint in `checkpoints/` (untracked \u2014 delete if you don't want it), and `black`/`isort`/`mypy` aren't installed in `surya_ws`, so I checked the 100-char limit by hand instead. All over-length lines are pre-existing.",
